WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:01.500
- You are going to learn

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:04.890
about positive communication
skills for leaders.

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:08.037
I'm here with my good friend
and co-author, Julien Mirivel,

00:00:08.037 --> 00:00:10.860
and we're gonna talk to
you all about some content

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:12.577
from our recent book, also called,

00:00:12.577 --> 00:00:14.400
"Positive Communication for Leaders".

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.710
We wrote this book and we're
making this video series,

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:18.600
because now more than ever,

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:21.180
leaders need to be able to
connect with their followers,

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:24.120
their employees, to build relationships,

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:28.860
and help cultivate a team
that's cohesive and effective.

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:30.750
In fact, we're doing a seven-part series.

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:33.360
This is video number one,
where we're gonna break down

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:37.140
all of the behaviors in this
model of positive communication

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:39.300
that Julien came up
with several years back,

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:41.280
and we've now built a book around it

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:43.770
on leadership and this video series,

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:45.480
so let's get right into it.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:48.150
Julien, what is a positive leader?

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:51.240
- Well, you know, positive
leadership is all about dreaming

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:52.950
about the people who inspire us,

00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:55.650
and we want people who
are watching this video

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:59.490
to feel it, too, so try to
do this exercise with us.

00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:02.910
Think about one of the
most inspirational leaders

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:07.290
that you have worked with in
your own professional career.

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:10.560
Try to identify someone specific,

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:12.990
somebody who made a
real positive difference

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:14.940
in the workplace for you.

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:16.950
What was it like to work for them?

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:20.010
What emotions did they bring out of you?

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:24.510
And what did they specifically
do to make you feel this way?

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:26.610
What are the aspect of their leadership

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:30.030
that really encouraged excellence in you,

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:32.520
but maybe also other people around you?

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:34.980
And if you can think of specific habits,

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:36.840
maybe personality traits,

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:39.810
but more importantly, core
behaviors, what they did,

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:41.670
then perhaps you can identify

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:45.000
what we're going to be
describing in these seven videos.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.520
- And we'd actually like you
to pause the video right now.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:49.110
If you don't have
someone specific in mind,

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:50.130
it's really important that you think

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:52.380
of a specific inspirational leader

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:53.640
who you've worked directly with

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:55.800
in the past in one way or another.

00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:58.320
The goal, of course, is
not to just talk about

00:01:58.320 --> 00:01:59.820
this inspirational leader in your life,

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:02.250
but to help you become one, too.

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:03.930
- We don't know what you wrote down.

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:05.550
We don't know who you're thinking about.

00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:08.370
We don't know, maybe not
even know that person,

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:10.350
but we believe that
there are some elements

00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:13.860
of inspirational leadership
that transcends all leaders

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:16.410
who are doing this way and all cultures.

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:17.940
You know, we've tested this now,

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:20.790
this question in the
United States and Canada,

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:23.160
but also, in Saudi Arabia and Costa Rica,

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:24.690
a number of other cultures.

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:26.910
And we found that no matter where we go,

00:02:26.910 --> 00:02:30.120
inspirational leaders
have common qualities

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:32.550
no matter where we live and work.

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:35.220
So often those individuals are leaders

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:39.330
who make us feel valued, and
they make us feel respected.

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:42.990
These leaders are people
who create an environment,

00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:46.500
where we feel like we can thrive,
where we really empowered,

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:49.440
and obviously, they create
a great place to work,

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:51.480
an environment that's challenging,

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:53.700
but that's also encouraging
and safe for all.

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:55.860
In short, they are what Alex and I

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:57.750
would call a positive leader.

00:02:57.750 --> 00:03:01.980
They make choices every day
about how to be and how to act,

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:04.290
and those actions end up creating

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:06.390
an extraordinary workplace.

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:09.930
And what we wanna show you
is how you can do that, too.

00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:12.750
- So what Julien and I
are doing in this series

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:15.180
is we're looking at six
key communication behaviors

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:17.040
for leaders, and we're
gonna make a follow-up video

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:21.120
for each of those six behaviors,
where we drill down deeply.

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:23.940
Right now, we're going
to give you an overview

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:25.290
of these six behaviors,

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:26.880
so you get an overview of the overall

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:30.060
positive communication
model that Julien created,

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:32.190
and Julien's gonna start us off.

00:03:32.190 --> 00:03:35.970
- The first behavior of positive
communication is greeting.

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:39.870
Greet to create human contact.

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:40.800
The act of greeting,

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:43.590
we know acknowledges
that other people exist,

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:47.100
and it naturally moves us
in the direction of others.

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:50.760
And so as a leader, Alex
and I really encourage

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:53.760
great leaders to create lots
of connections with people,

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:55.830
and learn how to repair relationships

00:03:55.830 --> 00:03:57.300
when they're not doing well.

00:03:57.300 --> 00:03:59.850
When you choose to greet other people,

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:02.430
you also choose to
create great connections

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:04.320
and build relationships.

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:07.350
- The second behavior is asking questions.

00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:10.830
Ask questions to discover the unknown.

00:04:10.830 --> 00:04:14.250
The questions you ask
shape the responses you get

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:16.410
in conversations with other people.

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:18.510
And as a leader, you
need to place yourself

00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:20.340
in a position of discovery,

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:23.850
so that you can learn
about your followers' needs

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:26.010
and interests and wants and their world,

00:04:26.010 --> 00:04:27.870
so that you, as a leader, can adapt

00:04:27.870 --> 00:04:30.180
and enter their world more successfully.

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:34.380
You can lead essentially
through asking better questions.

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:36.900
That's a real concept that
is hard to understand,

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:39.750
because most leaders wanna
talk at their people,

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:41.100
and there's a time and a place for that.

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:44.220
But we want to coach you to
learn how to ask questions,

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:47.040
to develop relationships and
get to know people better.

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:49.770
- Third, positive
leaders give compliments.

00:04:49.770 --> 00:04:53.130
They use positive
communication to affect others.

00:04:53.130 --> 00:04:57.630
Complimenting others
affects their sense of self.

00:04:57.630 --> 00:04:59.310
We believe that what you say and do

00:04:59.310 --> 00:05:02.910
shapes not just who you are
and who other people are,

00:05:02.910 --> 00:05:06.510
but also fundamentally
shape who they become.

00:05:06.510 --> 00:05:09.360
And so as a leader, you can
use the power of communication

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:12.330
to affect others in a positive direction.

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:14.490
And the behavior we want
people thinking about

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:17.820
is complimenting and the
ability to give specific,

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:21.210
meaningful feedback to build people up,

00:05:21.210 --> 00:05:25.020
and strengthen the people
you work with along the way.

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:26.190
- Fourth is disclosure.

00:05:26.190 --> 00:05:30.060
Disclose more to deepen
your relationships.

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:32.220
Now, disclosure happens
anytime you reveal anything

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:34.050
about yourself to other people.

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:37.290
As a leader, that means
talking about who you are,

00:05:37.290 --> 00:05:39.210
maybe even outside of work a little bit.

00:05:39.210 --> 00:05:40.950
It's about revealing your opinions,

00:05:40.950 --> 00:05:42.930
stories about your background.

00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:45.300
It's about telling
people your expectations,

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:47.490
so they don't have to read your mind.

00:05:47.490 --> 00:05:50.940
It's about sharing gratitude
and expressing that gratitude,

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:52.350
because people may not realize

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:53.970
how grateful you are for them.

00:05:53.970 --> 00:05:57.090
Now, when you disclose, it
can feel a little vulnerable.

00:05:57.090 --> 00:05:59.580
And when we get into the
specific video about disclosure,

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:01.140
we'll address that.

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:02.370
But anytime you are disclosing

00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:03.570
and putting yourself out there,

00:06:03.570 --> 00:06:06.480
you're starting to
strengthen the relationships

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:08.370
that you've begun forming earlier.

00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:10.770
- The fifth behavior of
positive communication

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:12.570
is encouraging.

00:06:12.570 --> 00:06:17.190
Encourage your people to give support.

00:06:17.190 --> 00:06:20.220
Every workplace is going
to face challenges.

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:23.370
Every employee will have
moments of great times,

00:06:23.370 --> 00:06:25.800
a moment of really difficult time.

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:28.260
And what great leadership
is all about is finding ways

00:06:28.260 --> 00:06:31.110
of supporting the people
around you to succeed.

00:06:31.110 --> 00:06:33.600
Leadership is both managing crisis,

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:37.530
but also finding ways to motivate
and encouraging employees

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:39.990
at the right moment and at the right time.

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:42.720
When you choose to
communicate effectively,

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:45.540
what you're doing is you're
using communication as a gift,

00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:47.970
and then you have the ability
to provide the support

00:06:47.970 --> 00:06:51.810
that people need to succeed,
and we'll show you how.

00:06:51.810 --> 00:06:54.930
- The sixth part of
this model is listening.

00:06:54.930 --> 00:06:58.320
Improve how you listen
to transcend differences.

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:00.240
Now, no comprehensive model

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:02.340
would be complete without listening.

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:05.460
On a personal level, you need
to practice what we call,

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:08.130
deep listening to your
followers and your employees,

00:07:08.130 --> 00:07:10.170
so you can understand their needs.

00:07:10.170 --> 00:07:12.540
Those followers need to be heard

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:15.150
and you, as a leader,
have that responsibility.

00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:17.370
That means, of course,
removing distractions,

00:07:17.370 --> 00:07:20.940
and really 100% being all in
focused on your listening.

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:23.640
When you listen deeply like
that, you will transcend

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:26.100
the perceived differences
between you and others

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:28.440
and between and among your followers.

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:30.990
And that will help create more team unity,

00:07:30.990 --> 00:07:33.540
and bring people together
to create cohesiveness

00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:35.250
in your organization's culture.

00:07:35.250 --> 00:07:38.010
You find common ground,
and you begin to understand

00:07:38.010 --> 00:07:39.900
how you can work together.

00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:41.220
Those are the six behaviors.

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:43.380
That's an overview taken together,

00:07:43.380 --> 00:07:46.200
the six behaviors form
the model or the wheel

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:48.750
of positive communication for leaders.

00:07:48.750 --> 00:07:52.050
And as we asked earlier,
how many of these behaviors

00:07:52.050 --> 00:07:54.930
did that inspirational leader
that you thought of do?

00:07:54.930 --> 00:07:56.730
If you think about how they come across,

00:07:56.730 --> 00:07:58.620
how they communicate with you
and other people around them,

00:07:58.620 --> 00:08:00.780
we're wondering how
many of the six they do.

00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:03.210
It shows you how important these are.

00:08:03.210 --> 00:08:04.830
And over the next several
weeks, as I mentioned,

00:08:04.830 --> 00:08:06.600
we're gonna be putting
out six more videos,

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:09.090
one on each of these six behaviors

00:08:09.090 --> 00:08:11.190
that are gonna drill down more deeply,

00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:13.380
and coach you through
exactly how to do them,

00:08:13.380 --> 00:08:15.510
when to do them, even give you some good

00:08:15.510 --> 00:08:17.190
starter sentences and samples,

00:08:17.190 --> 00:08:19.650
so you can see exactly
how you can apply them.

00:08:19.650 --> 00:08:23.610
We're also putting out a free email class,

00:08:23.610 --> 00:08:26.910
a mini email class, a series
of nine emails that echo

00:08:26.910 --> 00:08:29.280
what you're gonna be learning
in this video series.

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:32.347
And, of course, you can
find our book on Amazon

00:08:32.347 --> 00:08:33.750
"Positive Communication for Leaders".

00:08:33.750 --> 00:08:36.660
We'll put links to all of that
in the expandable description

00:08:36.660 --> 00:08:38.130
below the video.

00:08:38.130 --> 00:08:40.650
- We'd love to hear how you
would answer the question

00:08:40.650 --> 00:08:43.050
we pose at the opening of the video,

00:08:43.050 --> 00:08:47.280
who is the most inspirational
leader who has affected you?

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:49.890
Can you please put that
in the common section?

00:08:49.890 --> 00:08:52.170
What emotions did they bring out of you?

00:08:52.170 --> 00:08:55.470
What did they specifically
do to make you feel that way?

00:08:55.470 --> 00:08:58.290
And what aspect of their leadership style

00:08:58.290 --> 00:09:00.660
encourage excellence in you?

00:09:00.660 --> 00:09:03.990
Leadership is about influencing others.

00:09:03.990 --> 00:09:06.450
And what we're really going
to show you is how to use

00:09:06.450 --> 00:09:08.550
the power of positive communication

00:09:08.550 --> 00:09:11.730
to inspire others effectively.

00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:14.820
Leaders like you can
be powerful role models

00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:16.740
to motivate others.

00:09:16.740 --> 00:09:18.660
- Be sure you tune in for the next video,

00:09:18.660 --> 00:09:21.210
where we're gonna show
you how to do greetings

00:09:21.210 --> 00:09:24.500
much more effectively, and show
you how powerful that can be

00:09:24.500 --> 00:09:27.870
in starting conversations
and starting relationships.

00:09:27.870 --> 00:09:28.740
Thanks for tuning in.

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:30.990
We look forward to reading
your comments below.

00:09:30.990 --> 00:09:33.588
Until next time, God bless, see you soon.

00:09:33.588 --> 00:09:36.171
(upbeat music)

00:09:39.510 --> 00:09:42.810
You are going to learn how
to greet people effectively

00:09:42.810 --> 00:09:44.310
in conversations.

00:09:44.310 --> 00:09:47.910
Good greetings are the fundamental
opening of every social

00:09:47.910 --> 00:09:50.010
and even professional conversation.

00:09:50.010 --> 00:09:52.650
Our focus is gonna be on
professional settings,

00:09:52.650 --> 00:09:54.210
but you can use good greetings

00:09:54.210 --> 00:09:55.950
in any interaction in your life,

00:09:55.950 --> 00:09:57.570
and I'm here with Dr. Julien Mirivel.

00:09:57.570 --> 00:09:59.587
We co-authored a book together called

00:09:59.587 --> 00:10:02.010
"Positive Communication for Leaders".

00:10:02.010 --> 00:10:04.050
It's built around a six-part model

00:10:04.050 --> 00:10:05.880
that Julien created years ago,

00:10:05.880 --> 00:10:08.790
and it's the backbone of this
video series and our book.

00:10:08.790 --> 00:10:11.250
In fact, this is video number two

00:10:11.250 --> 00:10:13.590
in a series of seven videos
that we're putting out

00:10:13.590 --> 00:10:14.550
over the next several weeks.

00:10:14.550 --> 00:10:16.950
We hope you stick around for all of it.

00:10:16.950 --> 00:10:20.220
Today, we're focusing on
greeting people with intention

00:10:20.220 --> 00:10:23.100
and all the power that comes
from opening conversations

00:10:23.100 --> 00:10:24.780
on the right foot like that.

00:10:24.780 --> 00:10:28.860
So first, why are intentional
greetings so important?

00:10:28.860 --> 00:10:30.990
- So Alex, greetings are important,

00:10:30.990 --> 00:10:34.260
because they at the heart
of human connection.

00:10:34.260 --> 00:10:36.330
Interactions that start with simple things

00:10:36.330 --> 00:10:38.250
like good morning, it's good to see you,

00:10:38.250 --> 00:10:40.590
or even an how are you,

00:10:40.590 --> 00:10:44.190
matters more in the world
of leadership than we think.

00:10:44.190 --> 00:10:45.870
When you greet other people,

00:10:45.870 --> 00:10:48.450
you literally acknowledge their existence.

00:10:48.450 --> 00:10:52.140
A greeting says, "I see you,
I know that you're there."

00:10:52.140 --> 00:10:55.500
And more importantly, it
cues to the other person,

00:10:55.500 --> 00:10:58.560
I want to connect with you.

00:10:58.560 --> 00:11:02.640
It's me moving in the direction of others.

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:04.980
So while greeting is a small behavior,

00:11:04.980 --> 00:11:08.640
we know that it can have
an outsize big impact,

00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:10.890
because the ultimate goal
of positive communication

00:11:10.890 --> 00:11:13.560
is to create "high quality connections",

00:11:13.560 --> 00:11:15.690
a word developed by Jane Dutton,

00:11:15.690 --> 00:11:18.150
a professor emerita at the business school

00:11:18.150 --> 00:11:19.830
at the University of Michigan.

00:11:19.830 --> 00:11:23.550
And these are short, positive
interactions that have a range

00:11:23.550 --> 00:11:27.570
of beneficial outcomes for
both people and their cultures.

00:11:27.570 --> 00:11:31.080
Greeting is moving in the
direction of other people,

00:11:31.080 --> 00:11:33.720
and making it possible to start

00:11:33.720 --> 00:11:35.730
those high quality connections.

00:11:35.730 --> 00:11:38.100
- Now, if you think
greetings are too easy,

00:11:38.100 --> 00:11:40.170
too simple to study and practice,

00:11:40.170 --> 00:11:43.800
I'd like to say more about how
a good greeting is much more

00:11:43.800 --> 00:11:45.300
than simply saying hello.

00:11:45.300 --> 00:11:47.160
A greeting serves a number of functions,

00:11:47.160 --> 00:11:48.900
especially in a professional setting.

00:11:48.900 --> 00:11:51.480
So greetings open up a
sequence of communication

00:11:51.480 --> 00:11:52.830
between two people.

00:11:52.830 --> 00:11:55.050
Someone has to make the first move.

00:11:55.050 --> 00:11:57.180
And if you've ever been
sitting there in a room,

00:11:57.180 --> 00:11:59.040
where people haven't greeted each other

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:00.030
and got to know each other,

00:12:00.030 --> 00:12:03.810
it can be very difficult to
feel comfortable around them,

00:12:03.810 --> 00:12:04.740
because they're unfamiliar.

00:12:04.740 --> 00:12:08.880
But a greeting starts the
communication sequence.

00:12:08.880 --> 00:12:12.000
Greetings also both
define and affirm people

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:14.100
and relationships.

00:12:14.100 --> 00:12:16.350
Moving in to say hello, when you do that,

00:12:16.350 --> 00:12:18.840
you're demonstrating that
you're open, approachable,

00:12:18.840 --> 00:12:21.990
you're willing to engage, that
you care about other people.

00:12:21.990 --> 00:12:25.410
This is one of the
reasons why when employees

00:12:25.410 --> 00:12:28.080
at the lower levels of
the pyramids, so to speak,

00:12:28.080 --> 00:12:31.320
feel so honored and cared for,

00:12:31.320 --> 00:12:33.660
when a high status leader
in the organization

00:12:33.660 --> 00:12:37.380
actually greets them, knows
their name, says hello,

00:12:37.380 --> 00:12:39.667
because it says to the
employee, the follower,

00:12:39.667 --> 00:12:41.850
"You matter, I care about you enough

00:12:41.850 --> 00:12:43.800
to pause for a minute and greet you."

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:45.360
And greetings, also, third,

00:12:45.360 --> 00:12:48.810
help you build a bridge to
achieve a specific purpose.

00:12:48.810 --> 00:12:50.820
So beyond the social connections,

00:12:50.820 --> 00:12:52.050
when you're in the workplace,

00:12:52.050 --> 00:12:54.930
you greet someone as just the
beginning, and it's understood

00:12:54.930 --> 00:12:57.360
that there's probably
some task collaboration

00:12:57.360 --> 00:12:59.670
that's going to follow that greeting.

00:12:59.670 --> 00:13:01.110
And you can't get to the task

00:13:01.110 --> 00:13:04.350
until you have a good
opening to the conversation.

00:13:04.350 --> 00:13:07.500
So a greeting just sets the
stage, it's just the beginning.

00:13:07.500 --> 00:13:08.580
But what happens, Julien,

00:13:08.580 --> 00:13:10.980
if we don't greet each
other with intention?

00:13:10.980 --> 00:13:12.690
Let's look at the other side of the coin.

00:13:12.690 --> 00:13:15.030
- So, you know, Alex, when
we don't greet each other

00:13:15.030 --> 00:13:16.740
with intentionality,

00:13:16.740 --> 00:13:20.040
first, what happens is the
relationship is hurt, right?

00:13:20.040 --> 00:13:22.950
Without communication,
there is no relationship.

00:13:22.950 --> 00:13:27.090
Without greeting, there's
no quality connection.

00:13:27.090 --> 00:13:29.250
But if we don't pay
attention to these details,

00:13:29.250 --> 00:13:31.260
interaction can quickly feel awkward.

00:13:31.260 --> 00:13:32.550
I'm sure you've been in a situation,

00:13:32.550 --> 00:13:34.200
where you say hello to somebody

00:13:34.200 --> 00:13:36.750
and the other person doesn't
respond, and suddenly,

00:13:36.750 --> 00:13:39.660
you feel, wow, what just took place here?

00:13:39.660 --> 00:13:41.670
You know, I feel totally alone

00:13:41.670 --> 00:13:43.830
and isolated from this encounter.

00:13:43.830 --> 00:13:45.030
A second thing that happens

00:13:45.030 --> 00:13:47.190
is it feels disrespectful, right?

00:13:47.190 --> 00:13:48.900
So if we're walking down the hallway

00:13:48.900 --> 00:13:50.880
and our boss is not even seeing us,

00:13:50.880 --> 00:13:52.650
is not even acknowledging us,

00:13:52.650 --> 00:13:55.170
we feel disrespected in some form.

00:13:55.170 --> 00:13:58.170
So being attentive to
that detail really matters

00:13:58.170 --> 00:13:59.940
in the world of leadership.

00:13:59.940 --> 00:14:03.180
And then finally, we have not caring.

00:14:03.180 --> 00:14:05.970
You know, most of us,
we want to be valued.

00:14:05.970 --> 00:14:09.257
We want to be loved in some
way, and greeting is the way,

00:14:09.257 --> 00:14:11.167
it's a small way of showing to people,

00:14:11.167 --> 00:14:12.600
"Hey, I care about you.

00:14:12.600 --> 00:14:14.370
I wanna see how you're doing."

00:14:14.370 --> 00:14:16.500
And when we pay attention to it,

00:14:16.500 --> 00:14:19.590
we can start to transform where
the relationships all about.

00:14:19.590 --> 00:14:23.160
Greetings, help us connect
with each other relationally,

00:14:23.160 --> 00:14:26.280
and they create a stage
for working together

00:14:26.280 --> 00:14:27.720
even more effectively.

00:14:27.720 --> 00:14:29.970
- Now, you probably know a
good greeting when you see it,

00:14:29.970 --> 00:14:31.440
but we're gonna spell it out for you,

00:14:31.440 --> 00:14:34.200
so that you can do it more
consistently and reliably.

00:14:34.200 --> 00:14:37.800
There are four elements of a
good greeting, here they are.

00:14:37.800 --> 00:14:40.020
First, it has to be authentic.

00:14:40.020 --> 00:14:42.660
You have to actually
care about other people.

00:14:42.660 --> 00:14:44.850
If you just give a default
automatic greeting,

00:14:44.850 --> 00:14:47.970
they're gonna feel that it
doesn't come across as genuine,

00:14:47.970 --> 00:14:52.080
so you have to muster up your
actual care for other people

00:14:52.080 --> 00:14:52.913
when you greet them.

00:14:52.913 --> 00:14:54.000
And then it'll feel more authentic,

00:14:54.000 --> 00:14:55.260
and you'll form a little connection.

00:14:55.260 --> 00:14:56.670
Second, you have to do something verbal.

00:14:56.670 --> 00:14:58.860
You have to say, hello, hey,
how you doing, good morning,

00:14:58.860 --> 00:15:00.120
how was your weekend?

00:15:00.120 --> 00:15:02.610
Verbalize your greeting,
don't just nod at them.

00:15:02.610 --> 00:15:05.040
But third, nonverbal is also important.

00:15:05.040 --> 00:15:06.750
You nod, you make eye contact,

00:15:06.750 --> 00:15:10.080
you pause and orient you
shoulders toward the other person.

00:15:10.080 --> 00:15:12.270
And four, be patient.

00:15:12.270 --> 00:15:14.520
Don't rush your greeting.

00:15:14.520 --> 00:15:17.430
You have to take that
extra couple of seconds

00:15:17.430 --> 00:15:19.080
to greet them effectively.

00:15:19.080 --> 00:15:20.640
We believe that greetings

00:15:20.640 --> 00:15:23.100
are an literal leadership behavior,

00:15:23.100 --> 00:15:24.367
because as we wrote in our book,

00:15:24.367 --> 00:15:25.867
"Positive Communication for Leaders",

00:15:25.867 --> 00:15:30.510
"It's a leader's responsibility
to initiate connection

00:15:30.510 --> 00:15:31.950
with their people."

00:15:31.950 --> 00:15:34.290
The leader, in other words, sets the tone.

00:15:34.290 --> 00:15:36.060
So Julien, tell us about your example

00:15:36.060 --> 00:15:37.710
you were sharing with me the other day.

00:15:37.710 --> 00:15:40.500
- Well, you know, I just
had a wonderful visit

00:15:40.500 --> 00:15:42.000
with a Japanese executive.

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:44.760
I was in Costa Rica, and
we just found each other

00:15:44.760 --> 00:15:46.140
really at a restaurant.

00:15:46.140 --> 00:15:48.210
And we ended up kind
of talking and engaging

00:15:48.210 --> 00:15:49.440
in conversation.

00:15:49.440 --> 00:15:50.640
And as I got to know him,

00:15:50.640 --> 00:15:53.100
he shared his whole leadership story.

00:15:53.100 --> 00:15:54.750
And really, it was one of sadness.

00:15:54.750 --> 00:15:56.910
He realized that he was isolated.

00:15:56.910 --> 00:15:58.770
He realized that he was not treating

00:15:58.770 --> 00:16:00.150
his people particularly well.

00:16:00.150 --> 00:16:02.820
In fact, he said, "You
know, for most of my career,

00:16:02.820 --> 00:16:04.410
when I would go to the office,

00:16:04.410 --> 00:16:06.240
I would walk straight through the hallway.

00:16:06.240 --> 00:16:07.330
I wouldn't even say hi to people.

00:16:07.330 --> 00:16:11.250
And I would go to my desk,
and straight to my PC."

00:16:11.250 --> 00:16:13.710
And even when people went
to see him in his office,

00:16:13.710 --> 00:16:16.050
he'd be working away
typing in the computer.

00:16:16.050 --> 00:16:18.450
And somebody would come and he
would say, "Okay, thank you."

00:16:18.450 --> 00:16:20.910
Barely make eye contact and barely engage.

00:16:20.910 --> 00:16:23.790
And he said, as a result of that approach,

00:16:23.790 --> 00:16:26.460
he lost a lot of respect.

00:16:26.460 --> 00:16:29.580
He lost a sense of connection to people

00:16:29.580 --> 00:16:31.020
and it had consequences.

00:16:31.020 --> 00:16:33.780
And he said, "Now, I'm
having a paradigm shift.

00:16:33.780 --> 00:16:38.190
I realize I cannot just be
a boss talking to objects.

00:16:38.190 --> 00:16:42.240
I have to be a leader
from person to person."

00:16:42.240 --> 00:16:45.150
And he started to implement
some of what we're describing,

00:16:45.150 --> 00:16:47.400
which is moving towards others,

00:16:47.400 --> 00:16:48.720
and asking how they're doing,

00:16:48.720 --> 00:16:50.610
and checking in with colleagues.

00:16:50.610 --> 00:16:52.230
And when people come into the office,

00:16:52.230 --> 00:16:54.270
then you respond positively

00:16:54.270 --> 00:16:56.220
to the first part of the greeting.

00:16:56.220 --> 00:17:00.600
And he noticed that those
relationships started to grow.

00:17:00.600 --> 00:17:01.680
And not only that,

00:17:01.680 --> 00:17:04.620
but his leadership
approach grew as a result.

00:17:04.620 --> 00:17:06.600
And his influence, of course.

00:17:06.600 --> 00:17:08.460
- Wonderful, so here is your challenge,

00:17:08.460 --> 00:17:10.350
your application for this video,

00:17:10.350 --> 00:17:13.560
anybody watching, so you
can get on the right path.

00:17:13.560 --> 00:17:16.620
First, make a list of two
people in your organization

00:17:16.620 --> 00:17:18.180
that you either don't know but you wanna,

00:17:18.180 --> 00:17:20.700
or that you know you need
to connect with more.

00:17:20.700 --> 00:17:25.560
Next, give each person a call
or a quick visit in person.

00:17:25.560 --> 00:17:27.900
You can do this on email if you must,

00:17:27.900 --> 00:17:30.510
but we really think
face-to-face or a phone call

00:17:30.510 --> 00:17:33.960
is the best way to start
forming a real connection.

00:17:33.960 --> 00:17:37.230
Next, give them your best
greeting, and simply tell them

00:17:37.230 --> 00:17:39.960
that you wanted to check in
and see how they were doing.

00:17:39.960 --> 00:17:42.450
Next, make a little bit of small talk,

00:17:42.450 --> 00:17:44.130
and then see where the
interaction takes you.

00:17:44.130 --> 00:17:45.660
And at the end of the conversation,

00:17:45.660 --> 00:17:47.280
there may be a need to follow up

00:17:47.280 --> 00:17:49.440
and you can make a plan
to follow up later.

00:17:49.440 --> 00:17:51.990
That's your challenge for this video.

00:17:51.990 --> 00:17:54.900
Start greeting people
well, do it with intention,

00:17:54.900 --> 00:17:58.050
and start with two people who
you can identify specifically.

00:17:58.050 --> 00:18:01.350
So this is the second video
in a series of seven videos

00:18:01.350 --> 00:18:02.970
on positive communication.

00:18:02.970 --> 00:18:05.400
For leaders, we invite you
to watch all seven videos.

00:18:05.400 --> 00:18:06.960
We're gonna put links to all of them

00:18:06.960 --> 00:18:09.150
once they're all posted
in the description below,

00:18:09.150 --> 00:18:12.210
along with a free email class series

00:18:12.210 --> 00:18:14.400
that you can sign up for
that mirrors or echoes

00:18:14.400 --> 00:18:16.830
and reinforces what we're
talking about in the videos.

00:18:16.830 --> 00:18:18.570
And, of course, you can pick up a copy

00:18:18.570 --> 00:18:20.850
of the book on Amazon if you'd like.

00:18:20.850 --> 00:18:23.880
Links to all of that is in the
description below the video.

00:18:23.880 --> 00:18:25.050
So we'll see you in the next video,

00:18:25.050 --> 00:18:26.550
where we're gonna talk
about the importance

00:18:26.550 --> 00:18:30.930
of asking questions, a
critical leadership behavior.

00:18:30.930 --> 00:18:32.310
We look forward to reading your comments

00:18:32.310 --> 00:18:33.210
in the comment section below.

00:18:33.210 --> 00:18:34.260
Until next time, thanks.

00:18:34.260 --> 00:18:36.041
God bless you, and we will see you soon.

00:18:36.041 --> 00:18:38.624
(upbeat music)

00:18:42.540 --> 00:18:44.460
Today, you're going to learn how to ask

00:18:44.460 --> 00:18:46.920
better questions in conversations.

00:18:46.920 --> 00:18:50.190
And I'm here with my good
friend, Dr. Julien Mirivel.

00:18:50.190 --> 00:18:52.200
We recently wrote a book together

00:18:52.200 --> 00:18:54.330
called, "Positive
Communication for Leaders".

00:18:54.330 --> 00:18:56.370
It's built around a six-part model

00:18:56.370 --> 00:18:58.410
that Julien created years ago,

00:18:58.410 --> 00:18:59.910
and we've recently wrote
this book around it.

00:18:59.910 --> 00:19:02.670
And we're also doing a
video series around it.

00:19:02.670 --> 00:19:05.910
This is video number three
in a seven-part series

00:19:05.910 --> 00:19:08.940
on each of the individual
behaviors in that model.

00:19:08.940 --> 00:19:10.470
And we're gonna get right to it today

00:19:10.470 --> 00:19:13.050
about how to ask good questions.

00:19:13.050 --> 00:19:14.640
So your first step as a leader

00:19:14.640 --> 00:19:17.340
is to commit to asking more questions.

00:19:17.340 --> 00:19:20.590
Julien, why should we ask more questions?

00:19:20.590 --> 00:19:22.920
- Well, you know, for
leaders asking questions

00:19:22.920 --> 00:19:23.820
is so important.

00:19:23.820 --> 00:19:27.720
It immediately puts you in
a position of discovery.

00:19:27.720 --> 00:19:30.210
And as leaders, we just
tend to get into the habit

00:19:30.210 --> 00:19:31.980
of just telling people what to do.

00:19:31.980 --> 00:19:34.410
We give lots of directives,

00:19:34.410 --> 00:19:37.590
and we acknowledge that
there's a time and a place

00:19:37.590 --> 00:19:39.900
for sharing information in that way.

00:19:39.900 --> 00:19:41.910
But what makes questions so powerful

00:19:41.910 --> 00:19:45.330
and so much more effective
is that they engage people,

00:19:45.330 --> 00:19:47.880
and they get the leaders to be placed

00:19:47.880 --> 00:19:49.740
in a position of discovery.

00:19:49.740 --> 00:19:53.310
And as a leader, questions
immediately accomplish that goal,

00:19:53.310 --> 00:19:58.310
and help you really lead to
concrete positive outcomes.

00:19:58.860 --> 00:20:01.110
Here's some facts that
Alex and I really like.

00:20:01.110 --> 00:20:02.220
You know, one of them is this.

00:20:02.220 --> 00:20:07.220
Only 6% of all acts of communication
are actually questions.

00:20:08.700 --> 00:20:12.240
But 60% of what we say

00:20:12.240 --> 00:20:14.970
is actually in response to a question.

00:20:14.970 --> 00:20:19.200
In other words, questions are
exponentially more engaging

00:20:19.200 --> 00:20:21.090
than just making statements.

00:20:21.090 --> 00:20:24.270
And research shows that
once we ask a question,

00:20:24.270 --> 00:20:27.840
our minds almost entirely
focus on trying to answer it.

00:20:27.840 --> 00:20:29.970
So what makes questions powerful, Alex,

00:20:29.970 --> 00:20:32.490
is that when you ask a question,

00:20:32.490 --> 00:20:36.660
you set in motion what
the next turn of talk

00:20:36.660 --> 00:20:39.660
is going to be about, what
other people are going to say,

00:20:39.660 --> 00:20:43.050
and that's gonna shape what
the interaction is all about.

00:20:43.050 --> 00:20:45.840
Good questions will give
followers and colleagues

00:20:45.840 --> 00:20:48.270
an opportunity to voice their opinions,

00:20:48.270 --> 00:20:51.030
but it's also gonna
show them that you care

00:20:51.030 --> 00:20:54.360
about their views and about them as people

00:20:54.360 --> 00:20:57.390
- Asking questions is a
fundamental leadership behavior.

00:20:57.390 --> 00:21:00.990
In fact, there are lots of
benefits to asking questions

00:21:00.990 --> 00:21:02.850
versus simply making statements.

00:21:02.850 --> 00:21:04.800
In fact, the benefits are
really hard to believe.

00:21:04.800 --> 00:21:06.570
So simply by asking more questions,

00:21:06.570 --> 00:21:08.910
here's what you benefit from.

00:21:08.910 --> 00:21:11.820
Individuals who ask
questions and listen deeply

00:21:11.820 --> 00:21:15.420
are more likely to be
perceived as leaders.

00:21:15.420 --> 00:21:17.700
Leaders who ask meaningful
questions for input

00:21:17.700 --> 00:21:20.820
are seen by others as
increasing effectiveness.

00:21:20.820 --> 00:21:22.980
Leaders who ask meaningful
and respectful questions

00:21:22.980 --> 00:21:25.740
create more encouraging
work relationships.

00:21:25.740 --> 00:21:27.330
And leaders who ask good questions

00:21:27.330 --> 00:21:30.270
successfully motivate their employees.

00:21:30.270 --> 00:21:33.870
So given all of these
benefits, we're asking you,

00:21:33.870 --> 00:21:36.750
start asking more questions
in the next 24 hours

00:21:36.750 --> 00:21:38.487
after watching this video.

00:21:38.487 --> 00:21:39.867
And the more questions you ask,

00:21:39.867 --> 00:21:41.640
the more repetitions you'll get,

00:21:41.640 --> 00:21:44.670
and you will get better at
this the more you do it.

00:21:44.670 --> 00:21:47.460
So the second tip, in addition
to asking more questions

00:21:47.460 --> 00:21:50.760
is to improve the quality
of the questions you ask.

00:21:50.760 --> 00:21:52.350
Julien, how can we do that?

00:21:52.350 --> 00:21:54.750
How can we ask better questions?

00:21:54.750 --> 00:21:57.300
- So what we show in the book
and what we really want you

00:21:57.300 --> 00:22:00.720
to learn in today's video is
how to flip your questions,

00:22:00.720 --> 00:22:03.810
to move from asking close-ended questions,

00:22:03.810 --> 00:22:06.420
to asking open-ended questions.

00:22:06.420 --> 00:22:07.860
And there's a big difference.

00:22:07.860 --> 00:22:09.870
Open-ended questions typically start

00:22:09.870 --> 00:22:14.130
with words like how, what, or why.

00:22:14.130 --> 00:22:17.220
Close-ended questions tend
to start with words like,

00:22:17.220 --> 00:22:20.190
are you, will you, did you.

00:22:20.190 --> 00:22:22.230
And you'll notice that
closed-end questions

00:22:22.230 --> 00:22:24.600
control and narrow the interaction

00:22:24.600 --> 00:22:25.800
versus open-ended questions,

00:22:25.800 --> 00:22:28.440
which kind of given an
expansiveness, right?

00:22:28.440 --> 00:22:30.810
A breadth of answers.

00:22:30.810 --> 00:22:31.920
Let me give you a scenario.

00:22:31.920 --> 00:22:33.630
So you come to work on a Monday morning,

00:22:33.630 --> 00:22:36.187
you go see a colleague, and you ask,

00:22:36.187 --> 00:22:38.850
"Did you have a fun this weekend?"

00:22:38.850 --> 00:22:41.610
And the person may say,
"Yeah, how about you?"

00:22:41.610 --> 00:22:43.650
And you notice that's
a close-ended question,

00:22:43.650 --> 00:22:45.480
did you have fun this weekend,

00:22:45.480 --> 00:22:47.100
and the answer you got is very short.

00:22:47.100 --> 00:22:50.430
In fact, you've learned
nothing about your colleague,

00:22:50.430 --> 00:22:53.610
what they enjoy doing, or
even about their weekends.

00:22:53.610 --> 00:22:56.310
Close-ended questions can be
acceptable as a starting point,

00:22:56.310 --> 00:22:58.080
but you notice that for leaders,

00:22:58.080 --> 00:23:00.420
they need to learn to
ask open-ended questions,

00:23:00.420 --> 00:23:02.580
'cause that's where the connection is,

00:23:02.580 --> 00:23:04.950
and that's where you can
learn from other people.

00:23:04.950 --> 00:23:07.500
So take the same situation,
it's a Monday morning,

00:23:07.500 --> 00:23:09.420
you go to work to see a colleague.

00:23:09.420 --> 00:23:12.810
What if instead of asking,
did you have a fun weekend,

00:23:12.810 --> 00:23:14.250
you flip the question,

00:23:14.250 --> 00:23:17.430
and just ask, "What did
you do this weekend?"

00:23:17.430 --> 00:23:19.140
And you'll notice the person may respond

00:23:19.140 --> 00:23:21.360
with something like this, "Oh,
I did all sorts of things.

00:23:21.360 --> 00:23:23.850
You know, I went to the beach
on Saturday with my family,

00:23:23.850 --> 00:23:26.280
then on Sunday, we cooked
a great meal together."

00:23:26.280 --> 00:23:28.050
And they may share even more information.

00:23:28.050 --> 00:23:30.297
And the more they share,
the more you'll learn.

00:23:30.297 --> 00:23:31.740
And the more you'll learn,

00:23:31.740 --> 00:23:33.960
the more you'll have understanding,

00:23:33.960 --> 00:23:36.390
and that's what you want as a leader.

00:23:36.390 --> 00:23:39.000
- Open-ended questions
virtually always lead

00:23:39.000 --> 00:23:42.900
to a more robust conversation
than close-ended questions.

00:23:42.900 --> 00:23:45.720
So here's some sample open-ended questions

00:23:45.720 --> 00:23:46.980
that you can ask your followers.

00:23:46.980 --> 00:23:49.290
Here are the questions
you might ask to discover

00:23:49.290 --> 00:23:51.367
what your followers think, for example,

00:23:51.367 --> 00:23:53.347
"What is your opinion on this?"

00:23:53.347 --> 00:23:55.867
"How do you feel about X?"

00:23:55.867 --> 00:23:58.770
"What do you see as the next steps?"

00:23:58.770 --> 00:24:00.990
Here's some other samples
you can ask your followers

00:24:00.990 --> 00:24:04.560
if you wanna discover
more about them outside

00:24:04.560 --> 00:24:06.300
of their role at work.

00:24:06.300 --> 00:24:08.647
You could ask, "Tell me your story."

00:24:08.647 --> 00:24:11.497
"What are some of your personal
and professional goals?"

00:24:11.497 --> 00:24:13.867
"Who are your role models?"

00:24:13.867 --> 00:24:17.220
"When do you feel truly
energized and engaged at work?"

00:24:17.220 --> 00:24:18.930
I recently asked one of these questions

00:24:18.930 --> 00:24:21.570
when I was out to dinner with Julien.

00:24:21.570 --> 00:24:24.990
We were both presenting to
a client in another city,

00:24:24.990 --> 00:24:26.310
and the client took us out to dinner

00:24:26.310 --> 00:24:28.560
the night before our presentation,

00:24:28.560 --> 00:24:31.200
and after we ordered our food,
we had some time to talk.

00:24:31.200 --> 00:24:34.920
And I simply asked each of our
hosts, "Tell me your story."

00:24:34.920 --> 00:24:36.930
And they said, "Well, what
do you mean, what story?"

00:24:36.930 --> 00:24:39.240
And I said, "Your whole life story."

00:24:39.240 --> 00:24:41.070
And for the next 45 minutes to an hour,

00:24:41.070 --> 00:24:42.240
the four of us went around,

00:24:42.240 --> 00:24:44.760
and basically told our life story,

00:24:44.760 --> 00:24:46.080
short versions of our life story.

00:24:46.080 --> 00:24:49.410
In fact, one of the people
went back about 400 years

00:24:49.410 --> 00:24:53.400
to when their ancestors first
arrived in the United States.

00:24:53.400 --> 00:24:55.290
And you could feel as
the conversation went on,

00:24:55.290 --> 00:24:58.320
we were discovering the
unknown about each other

00:24:58.320 --> 00:25:01.950
and our relationship was
deepening with each talking turn.

00:25:01.950 --> 00:25:04.650
It was really a fantastic conversation

00:25:04.650 --> 00:25:06.750
and the person the next day mentioned

00:25:06.750 --> 00:25:09.750
what a powerful interaction it was.

00:25:09.750 --> 00:25:11.040
So Julien, why don't you tell us about

00:25:11.040 --> 00:25:12.810
the next steps for this video?

00:25:12.810 --> 00:25:14.910
- Yeah, we'd like you to
take a few next steps,

00:25:14.910 --> 00:25:17.280
and the first one is if you have used

00:25:17.280 --> 00:25:20.610
the great open-ended questions
that has worked for you,

00:25:20.610 --> 00:25:21.990
put it in the comments below,

00:25:21.990 --> 00:25:23.880
so we can all learn from each other,

00:25:23.880 --> 00:25:26.610
and have lots of examples of
great open-ended questions

00:25:26.610 --> 00:25:29.670
to open up our conversations with others.

00:25:29.670 --> 00:25:32.310
But the second thing we would
like you to do as a next step

00:25:32.310 --> 00:25:36.690
is to commit to asking more
questions, better questions,

00:25:36.690 --> 00:25:39.420
and more open questions
in the next 24 hours.

00:25:39.420 --> 00:25:42.990
We want you to feel and see the difference

00:25:42.990 --> 00:25:45.180
if you move from asking
close-ended questions

00:25:45.180 --> 00:25:48.360
to asking open-ended questions
in your interactions.

00:25:48.360 --> 00:25:50.040
So specifically, what we'd like you to do

00:25:50.040 --> 00:25:54.060
is go meet with a follower, or
a colleague, or an employee,

00:25:54.060 --> 00:25:58.620
and ask them three meaningful
open-ended questions

00:25:58.620 --> 00:26:00.900
to position yourself
in a mode of discovery.

00:26:00.900 --> 00:26:04.170
Make this whole intention
about getting to know them,

00:26:04.170 --> 00:26:06.240
and getting to understand
their perspective

00:26:06.240 --> 00:26:07.530
and their worldview.

00:26:07.530 --> 00:26:09.630
And you'll notice that when you do that,

00:26:09.630 --> 00:26:12.780
something really magical
happens in the interaction.

00:26:12.780 --> 00:26:14.197
As we say in our book,

00:26:14.197 --> 00:26:16.620
"It's up to leaders to position themselves

00:26:16.620 --> 00:26:18.270
to ask better questions

00:26:18.270 --> 00:26:20.670
and to discover what people have to say."

00:26:20.670 --> 00:26:21.990
And when you do that,

00:26:21.990 --> 00:26:24.030
you're gonna experience
something completely different

00:26:24.030 --> 00:26:25.860
in the realm of your relationships,

00:26:25.860 --> 00:26:27.960
both even at work and at home.

00:26:27.960 --> 00:26:29.640
- This is the third video in this series

00:26:29.640 --> 00:26:32.610
of seven for communication
for positive leaders.

00:26:32.610 --> 00:26:34.830
We encourage you to stick
with the entire series.

00:26:34.830 --> 00:26:36.450
We're gonna post all of the videos

00:26:36.450 --> 00:26:38.120
once they're all posted out there

00:26:38.120 --> 00:26:40.950
in the expandable
description below the video.

00:26:40.950 --> 00:26:43.770
You can also sign up for
the free mini email class

00:26:43.770 --> 00:26:47.070
that mirrors what we're going
through in these videos.

00:26:47.070 --> 00:26:48.450
And, of course, you can pick up a copy

00:26:48.450 --> 00:26:50.910
of our new book at Amazon.

00:26:50.910 --> 00:26:53.400
Links to all of that are
in the description below.

00:26:53.400 --> 00:26:55.590
We look forward to reading your comments,

00:26:55.590 --> 00:26:56.970
and in the next video, we're gonna work

00:26:56.970 --> 00:26:58.740
on how to give compliments,

00:26:58.740 --> 00:27:01.890
another key behavior for positive leaders.

00:27:01.890 --> 00:27:03.307
We'll see you then, bye-Bye.

00:27:03.307 --> 00:27:05.890
(upbeat music)

00:27:09.840 --> 00:27:13.440
Today, you're going to learn
how to give positive feedback,

00:27:13.440 --> 00:27:16.290
specifically how to give
genuine compliments.

00:27:16.290 --> 00:27:18.450
This works in a workplace situation,

00:27:18.450 --> 00:27:20.760
but works really in any
kind of relationship.

00:27:20.760 --> 00:27:24.090
And we are in video number
four in a seven-part series.

00:27:24.090 --> 00:27:26.580
I'm here with my good
friend, Dr. Julien Mirivel.

00:27:26.580 --> 00:27:28.050
We co-wrote a book together

00:27:28.050 --> 00:27:29.667
called, "Positive
Communication for Leaders".

00:27:29.667 --> 00:27:32.670
And this video series
is based upon that book,

00:27:32.670 --> 00:27:35.670
which is based upon a model
of positive communication

00:27:35.670 --> 00:27:38.490
that Julien developed several years back.

00:27:38.490 --> 00:27:41.370
In part, leadership is about
creating an environment

00:27:41.370 --> 00:27:44.337
in which people feel valued,
respected, and engaged.

00:27:44.337 --> 00:27:47.010
And one of the best ways to create that

00:27:47.010 --> 00:27:49.680
is by giving more positive feedback.

00:27:49.680 --> 00:27:51.120
Our approach in this video is to show you

00:27:51.120 --> 00:27:54.750
how to give genuine compliments,
to build relationships,

00:27:54.750 --> 00:27:58.500
and enhance employee and
organizational performance.

00:27:58.500 --> 00:28:00.990
Complimenting is one of
the six key behaviors

00:28:00.990 --> 00:28:04.680
in Julien's positive
model of communication.

00:28:04.680 --> 00:28:06.720
- In the model of positive communication,

00:28:06.720 --> 00:28:11.010
we argue that complimenting
affects people's sense of self.

00:28:11.010 --> 00:28:13.410
It affects not just who
they are in the moment,

00:28:13.410 --> 00:28:16.260
their identity, but it
more importantly impacts

00:28:16.260 --> 00:28:18.060
who they're going to become.

00:28:18.060 --> 00:28:21.540
Complimenting is a powerful
form of giving positive feedback

00:28:21.540 --> 00:28:23.880
because one, it's the most direct way

00:28:23.880 --> 00:28:28.080
of telling another person who
they are and how we see them.

00:28:28.080 --> 00:28:31.320
It also identifies
specifically good performance

00:28:31.320 --> 00:28:34.950
or great performance, and then
it shapes the other person,

00:28:34.950 --> 00:28:37.620
their sense of self, their identities.

00:28:37.620 --> 00:28:40.590
Positive feedback,
complimenting, and recognition

00:28:40.590 --> 00:28:43.740
drive toward lots of benefits for leaders,

00:28:43.740 --> 00:28:45.990
many of which we mention in the book.

00:28:45.990 --> 00:28:49.980
Consider several facts, from
the most up-to-date research

00:28:49.980 --> 00:28:51.480
we have on this topic.

00:28:51.480 --> 00:28:55.110
The most compelling driver
of an employee's performance

00:28:55.110 --> 00:28:58.170
is the quality of the
relationship that they have

00:28:58.170 --> 00:29:00.240
with their immediate supervisor.

00:29:00.240 --> 00:29:04.470
Focusing on strength is
going to improve performance,

00:29:04.470 --> 00:29:06.870
whereas, focusing on weaknesses,

00:29:06.870 --> 00:29:09.390
which is going to undermine performance.

00:29:09.390 --> 00:29:12.630
And then finally, employees
consider recognition

00:29:12.630 --> 00:29:15.690
for a job well done, respectful treatment,

00:29:15.690 --> 00:29:18.690
and coaching and feedback
more important than pay

00:29:18.690 --> 00:29:20.130
across the board.

00:29:20.130 --> 00:29:22.140
- Let's make this even
more personal right now.

00:29:22.140 --> 00:29:26.280
Take a moment to reflect on
your experience as an employee,

00:29:26.280 --> 00:29:27.780
some kind of follower.

00:29:27.780 --> 00:29:30.630
When you've been in that
role, what is one compliment

00:29:30.630 --> 00:29:32.400
you've received in your
professional career

00:29:32.400 --> 00:29:34.830
that has meant a great deal to you?

00:29:34.830 --> 00:29:37.730
Who gave you this compliment,
and how did it affect you

00:29:37.730 --> 00:29:40.470
in the short term and the long term?

00:29:40.470 --> 00:29:42.990
And you may even wanna
take a moment to write down

00:29:42.990 --> 00:29:46.140
exactly what was said,
so you can see the words,

00:29:46.140 --> 00:29:47.310
see the message.

00:29:47.310 --> 00:29:49.980
You may notice that as you remember this,

00:29:49.980 --> 00:29:52.290
and write it down, and
bring it to mind right now,

00:29:52.290 --> 00:29:55.500
It can start to create some
of the positive feelings

00:29:55.500 --> 00:29:57.780
that you felt at the time.

00:29:57.780 --> 00:30:00.270
- Let's give your own illustration.

00:30:00.270 --> 00:30:02.010
Here's a compliment that I received

00:30:02.010 --> 00:30:04.560
that made a very positive impact on me

00:30:04.560 --> 00:30:06.480
and my professional development.

00:30:06.480 --> 00:30:09.960
Years ago, when I was a young
professor at the university,

00:30:09.960 --> 00:30:12.270
on a Friday afternoon around four o'clock,

00:30:12.270 --> 00:30:14.070
I received a phone call.

00:30:14.070 --> 00:30:14.970
I picked up the phone,

00:30:14.970 --> 00:30:16.530
and I said, "Hi, this is Julien Mirivel.

00:30:16.530 --> 00:30:18.000
How can I help you?"

00:30:18.000 --> 00:30:20.670
On the other hand was David Belcher,

00:30:20.670 --> 00:30:23.490
who was at the time, the
provost of the university,

00:30:23.490 --> 00:30:27.180
the chief academic officer,
and he's giving me a call.

00:30:27.180 --> 00:30:29.550
He says, "You know Julien, I'm
about to become chancellor,

00:30:29.550 --> 00:30:32.430
and I'm moving and transitioning
to a new university,

00:30:32.430 --> 00:30:34.650
but I wanted to make a
few phone calls to people

00:30:34.650 --> 00:30:36.750
I really believe in on campus.

00:30:36.750 --> 00:30:39.510
And I want you to know that
you're an extraordinary teacher,

00:30:39.510 --> 00:30:41.130
an extraordinary scholar,

00:30:41.130 --> 00:30:43.710
and you are going to be an amazing leader

00:30:43.710 --> 00:30:45.420
for this institution.

00:30:45.420 --> 00:30:47.370
I cannot wait to see what you're gonna do

00:30:47.370 --> 00:30:48.960
in the next 10 years."

00:30:48.960 --> 00:30:51.570
That compliment really impacted me,

00:30:51.570 --> 00:30:54.480
not just in that moment
but for years to come.

00:30:54.480 --> 00:30:56.790
- And you can see how a sincere compliment

00:30:56.790 --> 00:31:00.240
from David Belcher influenced Julien.

00:31:00.240 --> 00:31:02.280
He remembers it to this day.

00:31:02.280 --> 00:31:04.350
It wasn't just shaping
how he felt in the moment,

00:31:04.350 --> 00:31:07.230
it shaped how he saw himself
moving forward in the future.

00:31:07.230 --> 00:31:09.600
And in the same way, now
imagine the kind of impact

00:31:09.600 --> 00:31:13.050
you can have by giving sincere
compliments to the followers

00:31:13.050 --> 00:31:14.700
and employees who you work with.

00:31:14.700 --> 00:31:17.190
The bottom line of this
video is we want you to start

00:31:17.190 --> 00:31:19.860
giving more compliments
to the people around you.

00:31:19.860 --> 00:31:21.930
Look for ways to reach out,

00:31:21.930 --> 00:31:24.120
and give them some positive
feedback like that.

00:31:24.120 --> 00:31:25.560
So what can you give compliments about?

00:31:25.560 --> 00:31:28.290
Let's give it even more
hands-on for you right now.

00:31:28.290 --> 00:31:30.540
Start by focusing on two key areas,

00:31:30.540 --> 00:31:31.830
where people generally want

00:31:31.830 --> 00:31:33.930
to feel those compliments come their way.

00:31:33.930 --> 00:31:35.700
The first is that we all have a need

00:31:35.700 --> 00:31:38.130
to be seen by others as competent.

00:31:38.130 --> 00:31:40.500
The second is, we need
to feel appreciated,

00:31:40.500 --> 00:31:42.240
liked, and valued.

00:31:42.240 --> 00:31:44.160
So those are two areas that you can give

00:31:44.160 --> 00:31:45.750
other people compliments.

00:31:45.750 --> 00:31:47.730
So right now, think of an actual person

00:31:47.730 --> 00:31:48.720
in your professional life.

00:31:48.720 --> 00:31:51.750
Reflect on when you have
seen them at their best

00:31:51.750 --> 00:31:53.700
in terms of what they
might be demonstrating

00:31:53.700 --> 00:31:54.960
their competence for.

00:31:54.960 --> 00:31:56.460
So maybe you saw them, for example,

00:31:56.460 --> 00:31:58.290
doing a great presentation.

00:31:58.290 --> 00:32:01.440
Perhaps, you saw them handle
a difficult situation well

00:32:01.440 --> 00:32:02.550
during a meeting.

00:32:02.550 --> 00:32:04.740
Now, in the same way,
think about a compliment

00:32:04.740 --> 00:32:06.780
that you may communicate to them

00:32:06.780 --> 00:32:09.810
that shows that you like
them and appreciate them.

00:32:09.810 --> 00:32:12.900
Now, keep in mind, you have to
give appropriate compliments.

00:32:12.900 --> 00:32:15.900
This is a workplace setting after all.

00:32:15.900 --> 00:32:17.280
We don't recommend, for example,

00:32:17.280 --> 00:32:20.040
you give other people compliments
about their appearance.

00:32:20.040 --> 00:32:22.203
That can often come off the wrong way,

00:32:23.040 --> 00:32:25.620
but you can give them
compliments about how you feel

00:32:25.620 --> 00:32:27.510
about them in terms of
your appreciate them

00:32:27.510 --> 00:32:28.530
and you like them.

00:32:28.530 --> 00:32:30.900
For example, Julien just
the other day sent me a text

00:32:30.900 --> 00:32:33.090
about how much he enjoys working

00:32:33.090 --> 00:32:36.270
on this video series together
and on our book together

00:32:36.270 --> 00:32:38.610
that we collaborated
on for a couple years.

00:32:38.610 --> 00:32:40.140
He's really good at that.

00:32:40.140 --> 00:32:43.020
So the key to these
compliments is being sincere.

00:32:43.020 --> 00:32:45.870
This can't be calculated
like you're doing it

00:32:45.870 --> 00:32:47.520
to get something from them.

00:32:47.520 --> 00:32:50.190
Genuine compliments
are the most direct way

00:32:50.190 --> 00:32:52.230
to give positive feedback to people,

00:32:52.230 --> 00:32:54.720
and shape how they see themselves.

00:32:54.720 --> 00:32:57.750
- Let's take some action today.

00:32:57.750 --> 00:33:02.400
Step one, take a minute to
identify one person you work with

00:33:02.400 --> 00:33:04.830
that could really benefit
from a compliment.

00:33:04.830 --> 00:33:07.890
This person could be a peer, a supervisor,

00:33:07.890 --> 00:33:08.970
or simply a subordinate,

00:33:08.970 --> 00:33:10.830
somebody who you work with every day.

00:33:10.830 --> 00:33:14.220
Step two, consider
these questions to draft

00:33:14.220 --> 00:33:16.890
your very own meaningful compliment.

00:33:16.890 --> 00:33:19.560
When have you seen this
person at their best?

00:33:19.560 --> 00:33:23.220
When have you recently seen
this person do something special

00:33:23.220 --> 00:33:25.260
or even make an important contribution?

00:33:25.260 --> 00:33:27.780
And more importantly,
you can ask what strength

00:33:27.780 --> 00:33:30.990
do you see in them on a consistent basis?

00:33:30.990 --> 00:33:33.030
Once you've answered these questions,

00:33:33.030 --> 00:33:34.290
you move on to step three.

00:33:34.290 --> 00:33:36.600
You craft your compliment.

00:33:36.600 --> 00:33:38.220
When drafting your compliment,

00:33:38.220 --> 00:33:41.400
make sure to identify a specific example

00:33:41.400 --> 00:33:44.280
of when you saw the
person do something great,

00:33:44.280 --> 00:33:48.390
and explain how what they did
has led to positive outcome

00:33:48.390 --> 00:33:50.130
on the basis of their strength.

00:33:50.130 --> 00:33:51.300
Let me share with you a compliment

00:33:51.300 --> 00:33:53.310
that I've been drafting for Alex,

00:33:53.310 --> 00:33:54.720
who's really been influential

00:33:54.720 --> 00:33:56.970
in our ability to write the book,

00:33:56.970 --> 00:33:58.290
but also make great progress

00:33:58.290 --> 00:34:00.030
in our own personal
and professional lives.

00:34:00.030 --> 00:34:03.270
Working with you, Alex,
is an absolute highlight

00:34:03.270 --> 00:34:04.680
of my career.

00:34:04.680 --> 00:34:08.520
You're kind, you're
generous, you're ethical,

00:34:08.520 --> 00:34:11.550
and you're so energizing
in everything that you do.

00:34:11.550 --> 00:34:14.010
I'm inspired by what you
are doing and who you are

00:34:14.010 --> 00:34:17.550
as a person and a professional every day.

00:34:17.550 --> 00:34:20.010
And more importantly, you've
helped to make the book

00:34:20.010 --> 00:34:22.677
that we wrote together,
concrete and practical,

00:34:22.677 --> 00:34:25.800
and you always kept refining
it until it was something

00:34:25.800 --> 00:34:28.080
that we were both really proud of.

00:34:28.080 --> 00:34:30.900
In short, I'm thankful for our friendship,

00:34:30.900 --> 00:34:32.940
and I'm thankful for your leadership.

00:34:32.940 --> 00:34:34.410
Thanks, my friend.

00:34:34.410 --> 00:34:35.243
- Thank you.

00:34:35.243 --> 00:34:36.600
I don't wanna pause for too long here,

00:34:36.600 --> 00:34:37.590
'cause we're in the middle of a video,

00:34:37.590 --> 00:34:39.510
but I really appreciate that, Julien.

00:34:39.510 --> 00:34:41.670
- So once you've crafted your compliment,

00:34:41.670 --> 00:34:43.560
you have to move to step four,

00:34:43.560 --> 00:34:46.740
because we have to let
people know how we feel,

00:34:46.740 --> 00:34:47.573
and that's step four.

00:34:47.573 --> 00:34:50.430
Step four is deliver your compliment.

00:34:50.430 --> 00:34:54.120
Research shows that the most
meaningful way of delivering

00:34:54.120 --> 00:34:55.950
a compliment is to do it in person,

00:34:55.950 --> 00:34:57.630
because it's the most memorable.

00:34:57.630 --> 00:35:00.000
If you cannot do that,
if that's not possible,

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:01.680
feel free to express your compliment

00:35:01.680 --> 00:35:03.780
maybe in a personal written note.

00:35:03.780 --> 00:35:07.050
Either way, and as we explain in the book,

00:35:07.050 --> 00:35:09.870
the most important part of complimenting

00:35:09.870 --> 00:35:11.730
is communicating it.

00:35:11.730 --> 00:35:13.980
- Now, the reason we're
emphasizing that last point

00:35:13.980 --> 00:35:16.230
is because here's an interesting fact.

00:35:16.230 --> 00:35:20.460
Professionals often don't
compliment their people

00:35:20.460 --> 00:35:22.620
even when they think
positively about them,

00:35:22.620 --> 00:35:24.030
and they don't do it for three reasons.

00:35:24.030 --> 00:35:26.370
The first reason is they
think positive thoughts,

00:35:26.370 --> 00:35:28.950
but don't bother to say them out loud.

00:35:28.950 --> 00:35:31.440
Second reason is they
expect that the interaction

00:35:31.440 --> 00:35:33.630
will be awkward when they express it.

00:35:33.630 --> 00:35:35.220
And the third is they simply don't think

00:35:35.220 --> 00:35:38.780
that it's important enough
to acknowledge others aloud.

00:35:38.780 --> 00:35:42.450
In other words, you personally
may have to push yourself

00:35:42.450 --> 00:35:43.860
to compliment others.

00:35:43.860 --> 00:35:46.560
In a study, only 50% of the participants

00:35:46.560 --> 00:35:49.800
who actually prepared a written compliment

00:35:49.800 --> 00:35:51.510
followed through and delivered it,

00:35:51.510 --> 00:35:54.240
and we hope that you are in that 50%.

00:35:54.240 --> 00:35:58.470
So we're encouraging you to
actually deliver the compliment

00:35:58.470 --> 00:36:00.450
and begin to give more compliments

00:36:00.450 --> 00:36:02.550
as a result of watching this video.

00:36:02.550 --> 00:36:05.940
People don't have access
merely to what you think

00:36:05.940 --> 00:36:07.080
and how you feel.

00:36:07.080 --> 00:36:08.010
They can't see that.

00:36:08.010 --> 00:36:10.560
They only have access
to what you actually do

00:36:10.560 --> 00:36:12.930
and actually say aloud.

00:36:12.930 --> 00:36:15.990
And that's why communicating
the compliment is so important.

00:36:15.990 --> 00:36:19.500
So make sure you're in that
top 50% who communicate it.

00:36:19.500 --> 00:36:24.000
- As a leader, you need to
use every opportunity you have

00:36:24.000 --> 00:36:27.630
to strengthen the people you
work with, to build them up,

00:36:27.630 --> 00:36:30.090
and prepare them for their future.

00:36:30.090 --> 00:36:32.610
And we hope that using what
you're learning in this video,

00:36:32.610 --> 00:36:34.620
you can apply this principle,

00:36:34.620 --> 00:36:37.800
and seize those opportunities
at every moment.

00:36:37.800 --> 00:36:39.600
- This is the fourth video in a series

00:36:39.600 --> 00:36:41.670
of seven that we're making that break down

00:36:41.670 --> 00:36:43.800
all of these skills in the book

00:36:43.800 --> 00:36:45.510
on "Positive Communication for Leaders"

00:36:45.510 --> 00:36:47.160
that you can check out on Amazon.

00:36:47.160 --> 00:36:50.850
Also, there's a free
nine-day mini email class

00:36:50.850 --> 00:36:51.900
that you can sign up for.

00:36:51.900 --> 00:36:53.460
We'll put links to that,

00:36:53.460 --> 00:36:56.130
and please feel free to
leave comments below.

00:36:56.130 --> 00:36:58.350
We look forward to reading them.

00:36:58.350 --> 00:37:01.800
In the next video, we go into
the topic of self-disclosure

00:37:01.800 --> 00:37:02.880
for leaders in the workplace,

00:37:02.880 --> 00:37:04.590
which is a really challenging one.

00:37:04.590 --> 00:37:05.940
We hope you tune in for that.

00:37:05.940 --> 00:37:07.500
Until then, thanks, God bless.

00:37:07.500 --> 00:37:09.213
We'll see you soon.

00:37:09.213 --> 00:37:11.796
(upbeat music)

00:37:15.150 --> 00:37:18.030
Today, you're going to
learn how to be more open

00:37:18.030 --> 00:37:20.970
in the workplace through self-disclosure.

00:37:20.970 --> 00:37:22.890
Now, the focus here in the video

00:37:22.890 --> 00:37:25.650
is about professional
settings, but self-disclosure

00:37:25.650 --> 00:37:27.660
can help deepen any relationship.

00:37:27.660 --> 00:37:29.490
And I'm here with my good
friend and co-author,

00:37:29.490 --> 00:37:31.800
Dr. Julien Mirivel.

00:37:31.800 --> 00:37:33.180
Julien and I wrote a book together

00:37:33.180 --> 00:37:34.740
called "Positive
Communication for Leaders".

00:37:34.740 --> 00:37:37.260
It's the foundation of this video series.

00:37:37.260 --> 00:37:40.890
We're in video number
five of seven videos.

00:37:40.890 --> 00:37:42.690
Self-disclosure is one of the key

00:37:42.690 --> 00:37:44.877
six communication behaviors in our book,

00:37:44.877 --> 00:37:46.980
and it's a really important tool

00:37:46.980 --> 00:37:49.020
that should be in every leader's toolbox.

00:37:49.020 --> 00:37:51.540
And we'll put a link to the
book, the description below,

00:37:51.540 --> 00:37:54.450
as well as a link to the mini email class

00:37:54.450 --> 00:37:55.650
that you can sign up for.

00:37:55.650 --> 00:37:58.887
In our experience,
self-disclosure is the behavior

00:37:58.887 --> 00:38:00.720
and the positive communication model

00:38:00.720 --> 00:38:04.290
with which most people
are least comfortable.

00:38:04.290 --> 00:38:06.780
- In the model of positive communication,

00:38:06.780 --> 00:38:11.010
we propose that when
leaders choose to disclose,

00:38:11.010 --> 00:38:13.470
they naturally deepen the relationships

00:38:13.470 --> 00:38:15.270
that they have with other people.

00:38:15.270 --> 00:38:20.270
We know today that 52% of CEOs
feel lonely in their roles.

00:38:20.760 --> 00:38:23.100
We know from reports across the world now

00:38:23.100 --> 00:38:26.940
that people feel generally
lonely in the workplace,

00:38:26.940 --> 00:38:29.700
approximately one in four adults does so.

00:38:29.700 --> 00:38:32.850
And leadership positions in
general within organizations

00:38:32.850 --> 00:38:35.670
often do not foster the
kind of work environments,

00:38:35.670 --> 00:38:39.270
where friendship and social
intimacy are possible.

00:38:39.270 --> 00:38:42.420
And the only way out of
that loneliness is to learn

00:38:42.420 --> 00:38:44.220
to use the power of positive communication

00:38:44.220 --> 00:38:46.260
to connect more deeply with people.

00:38:46.260 --> 00:38:50.220
And disclosure is the
secret sauce for that goal.

00:38:50.220 --> 00:38:52.020
It's an important leadership behavior,

00:38:52.020 --> 00:38:55.110
because disclosure
deepens your relationships

00:38:55.110 --> 00:38:57.270
and your connections with other people.

00:38:57.270 --> 00:39:01.260
- So first, let's talk about
what disclosure is and is not.

00:39:01.260 --> 00:39:05.400
At a basic level, disclosure
is about revealing aspects

00:39:05.400 --> 00:39:07.860
about yourself, information about yourself

00:39:07.860 --> 00:39:10.440
that people would not
know unless you told them.

00:39:10.440 --> 00:39:13.770
Just the other day, for example,
a colleague of mine shared

00:39:13.770 --> 00:39:16.620
some of her long-term
professional goals with me

00:39:16.620 --> 00:39:19.080
that I would not have known
unless she talked about them.

00:39:19.080 --> 00:39:20.970
In the same way, even in
professional settings,

00:39:20.970 --> 00:39:22.710
disclosure can mean telling people about

00:39:22.710 --> 00:39:26.040
what you did over the weekend,
talking about your family,

00:39:26.040 --> 00:39:27.200
if you're comfortable with that,

00:39:27.200 --> 00:39:29.760
or sharing a bit about
your hobbies and interests

00:39:29.760 --> 00:39:31.050
outside of work.

00:39:31.050 --> 00:39:34.350
Disclosure really is about an endless list

00:39:34.350 --> 00:39:37.680
of potential activities or
information that you might share

00:39:37.680 --> 00:39:41.250
that show who you are, show
how you think, how you feel,

00:39:41.250 --> 00:39:43.590
and let other people get to know you.

00:39:43.590 --> 00:39:46.950
- Ideally, for relationships
to be deepened,

00:39:46.950 --> 00:39:50.400
disclosure needs to include
certain key features.

00:39:50.400 --> 00:39:52.830
It must be authentic and genuine.

00:39:52.830 --> 00:39:56.220
It has to be truthful and honest,

00:39:56.220 --> 00:39:57.960
and it needs to be spontaneous

00:39:57.960 --> 00:40:00.510
rather than strategic or calculated.

00:40:00.510 --> 00:40:03.870
And more importantly,
it has to be personal.

00:40:03.870 --> 00:40:05.940
When we disclose with these features,

00:40:05.940 --> 00:40:08.850
we get to know each other
as whole human beings,

00:40:08.850 --> 00:40:12.840
find common ground, and
learn to trust each other.

00:40:12.840 --> 00:40:15.270
- So here's what disclosure is not.

00:40:15.270 --> 00:40:18.240
In contrast to that list of positives,

00:40:18.240 --> 00:40:21.480
disclosure is not about airing
all of your dirty laundry,

00:40:21.480 --> 00:40:23.820
or getting too personal too fast,

00:40:23.820 --> 00:40:25.440
or telling other people information

00:40:25.440 --> 00:40:27.660
that's really not suitable
for the workplace.

00:40:27.660 --> 00:40:30.180
Additionally, we'd like to
be clear here that disclosure

00:40:30.180 --> 00:40:32.610
is not a superficial tactic that you use

00:40:32.610 --> 00:40:36.360
to get something from
somebody or to close a sale.

00:40:36.360 --> 00:40:39.750
You're not relating to
people in that strategic way.

00:40:39.750 --> 00:40:42.480
We assume that you genuinely value people,

00:40:42.480 --> 00:40:45.450
and have their best interest
in mind when you're disclosing.

00:40:45.450 --> 00:40:47.850
And naturally, you must
use your best judgment

00:40:47.850 --> 00:40:48.990
anytime you're disclosing,

00:40:48.990 --> 00:40:51.630
and adapt any advice we're
giving you to your situation

00:40:51.630 --> 00:40:52.890
and your personality.

00:40:52.890 --> 00:40:56.040
Second, let's talk about
how you can begin disclosing

00:40:56.040 --> 00:40:57.810
in helpful ways at work.

00:40:57.810 --> 00:41:00.060
- The easiest way to get started

00:41:00.060 --> 00:41:03.360
is to learn to tell your story.

00:41:03.360 --> 00:41:04.770
Here are a couple ways.

00:41:04.770 --> 00:41:08.640
One is to share information
about your own personal

00:41:08.640 --> 00:41:10.260
and professional journey.

00:41:10.260 --> 00:41:13.500
In most situations, the leader in the room

00:41:13.500 --> 00:41:16.380
has been through similar
experiences in the past,

00:41:16.380 --> 00:41:18.840
and telling others about the ups and downs

00:41:18.840 --> 00:41:22.620
of our experiences and your
experiences can be a great way

00:41:22.620 --> 00:41:24.360
to bond with other people.

00:41:24.360 --> 00:41:27.420
We know this may seem
straightforward, but the question is,

00:41:27.420 --> 00:41:28.830
are you doing it yet?

00:41:28.830 --> 00:41:32.460
After this video, we hope you
do, because sharing your story

00:41:32.460 --> 00:41:35.460
has lots and lots of
benefits for connection.

00:41:35.460 --> 00:41:38.610
For example, in healthcare settings,

00:41:38.610 --> 00:41:40.350
research has shown that doctors

00:41:40.350 --> 00:41:43.050
who reveal their own
experiences to patients

00:41:43.050 --> 00:41:45.780
improve not only patient satisfaction,

00:41:45.780 --> 00:41:48.690
but their adherence to
treatment and the likelihood

00:41:48.690 --> 00:41:52.590
that they would pursue legal
action and a bad scenario.

00:41:52.590 --> 00:41:55.020
In the realm of education,
we know that teachers

00:41:55.020 --> 00:41:57.300
who share their experiences with students,

00:41:57.300 --> 00:41:59.580
whether that's their academic hardships,

00:41:59.580 --> 00:42:02.100
or even stories about their lives,

00:42:02.100 --> 00:42:04.110
not only impact student satisfaction,

00:42:04.110 --> 00:42:06.720
but it literally improves
students' learning.

00:42:06.720 --> 00:42:09.090
Sharing your stories at the heart

00:42:09.090 --> 00:42:11.040
of how to create more trust

00:42:11.040 --> 00:42:13.257
and better working
relationships with people.

00:42:13.257 --> 00:42:15.690
And if sharing your story
makes you a little bit anxious,

00:42:15.690 --> 00:42:17.880
remember that you are the decider.

00:42:17.880 --> 00:42:20.940
You can decide what to
share and when not to share,

00:42:20.940 --> 00:42:23.790
and you can always use your best judgment.

00:42:23.790 --> 00:42:26.070
- Now, here's our pro tip on that topic,

00:42:26.070 --> 00:42:29.670
disclose a little bit and see
how it goes, test this out.

00:42:29.670 --> 00:42:31.590
So you reveal a little bit
of personal information,

00:42:31.590 --> 00:42:33.630
and see how the other person responds.

00:42:33.630 --> 00:42:37.050
If it goes well, then you
reveal a little bit more,

00:42:37.050 --> 00:42:39.930
over time, and at your
own level of comfort.

00:42:39.930 --> 00:42:41.730
So here's another way to self-disclose.

00:42:41.730 --> 00:42:44.250
You can share some of your foibles,

00:42:44.250 --> 00:42:45.860
and I'll explain what
that means in a moment.

00:42:45.860 --> 00:42:48.060
So this is gonna take a little courage.

00:42:48.060 --> 00:42:50.160
We're not used to talking
about our weaknesses,

00:42:50.160 --> 00:42:53.070
but consider disclosing some
of your small shortcomings

00:42:53.070 --> 00:42:54.570
or small weaknesses.

00:42:54.570 --> 00:42:56.060
People can really relate to this.

00:42:56.060 --> 00:42:58.470
In our book, we draw on the
writings of Allen Weiner.

00:42:58.470 --> 00:43:01.320
He's a fellow speaker,
author, and consultant,

00:43:01.320 --> 00:43:03.300
and he makes a habit out of talking about

00:43:03.300 --> 00:43:06.960
his small weaknesses or
foibles with his employees,

00:43:06.960 --> 00:43:09.570
but even really high
level executive clients

00:43:09.570 --> 00:43:12.487
that he works with in
Fortune 500 companies.

00:43:12.487 --> 00:43:14.610
- "A foible is a small weakness.

00:43:14.610 --> 00:43:17.580
When I talk about mine,
people talk about theirs.

00:43:17.580 --> 00:43:19.530
Sometimes, that helps
us make a connection.

00:43:19.530 --> 00:43:22.980
I think I've surprised a lot
of people by my willingness

00:43:22.980 --> 00:43:26.550
to share a weakness, and it's
helped them open up to me.

00:43:26.550 --> 00:43:28.800
The more 'unsuitable'
my weakness to a point,

00:43:28.800 --> 00:43:31.470
the more I have invited such a connection.

00:43:31.470 --> 00:43:34.177
And most of the time the
person I've been talking with

00:43:34.177 --> 00:43:35.880
[about my Weakness] will say,

00:43:35.880 --> 00:43:37.050
'Don't be so hard on yourself.

00:43:37.050 --> 00:43:39.000
I can get you one better.'"

00:43:39.000 --> 00:43:40.230
Drawing on this example,

00:43:40.230 --> 00:43:43.650
we know that talking
about our small weaknesses

00:43:43.650 --> 00:43:45.210
can humanize you,

00:43:45.210 --> 00:43:48.270
and it's going to make you
more approachable as a leader.

00:43:48.270 --> 00:43:50.850
It opens up the door for
others to do the same,

00:43:50.850 --> 00:43:53.730
because it liberates other people

00:43:53.730 --> 00:43:57.420
to hear you speak
genuinely about yourself.

00:43:57.420 --> 00:44:00.990
- And third, expect other
people to reciprocate.

00:44:00.990 --> 00:44:04.050
The most likely result
of your self-disclosure

00:44:04.050 --> 00:44:06.330
is that other people will do the same.

00:44:06.330 --> 00:44:09.270
In the field of communication,
we call this reciprocity.

00:44:09.270 --> 00:44:11.130
When you share information about yourself,

00:44:11.130 --> 00:44:13.800
the most likely result is
people will start to disclose

00:44:13.800 --> 00:44:16.560
a similar type of information
from their own lives.

00:44:16.560 --> 00:44:19.800
In our presentations, we talk
about this as a magic wand.

00:44:19.800 --> 00:44:23.940
Disclosure is the closest
thing we have to a magic wand

00:44:23.940 --> 00:44:26.250
in terms of a concept in communication.

00:44:26.250 --> 00:44:28.410
When you disclose other people almost

00:44:28.410 --> 00:44:29.670
automatically reciprocate,

00:44:29.670 --> 00:44:32.880
and you've probably felt
this dynamic in conversations

00:44:32.880 --> 00:44:36.060
with other people, and it creates
a real bonding experience.

00:44:36.060 --> 00:44:37.740
You can feel the relationship develop.

00:44:37.740 --> 00:44:39.600
It's almost like giving each other

00:44:39.600 --> 00:44:41.940
small holiday exchange gifts.

00:44:41.940 --> 00:44:44.100
You can feel your relationship bond

00:44:44.100 --> 00:44:46.290
over those disclosures and those gifts.

00:44:46.290 --> 00:44:47.760
And in fact, one of the ways we do this

00:44:47.760 --> 00:44:50.670
in our live presentations, is
we talk about ourselves a lot

00:44:50.670 --> 00:44:52.500
to help us get to know the
audience and vice versa.

00:44:52.500 --> 00:44:53.490
But one of the ways we do it

00:44:53.490 --> 00:44:56.190
is to show pictures of my two dogs.

00:44:56.190 --> 00:44:57.390
That's something for my personal life,

00:44:57.390 --> 00:44:58.527
it has nothing to do with work,

00:44:58.527 --> 00:45:00.270
and I put them up on the screen,

00:45:00.270 --> 00:45:02.400
and I tell a quick story about them,

00:45:02.400 --> 00:45:06.300
and we now stop our presentations
and give everybody there

00:45:06.300 --> 00:45:09.300
the opportunity to share
pictures of their dogs, as well.

00:45:09.300 --> 00:45:13.110
Because when I self-disclose
like a magic wand,

00:45:13.110 --> 00:45:16.830
they wanna reciprocate and
share about their own pets,

00:45:16.830 --> 00:45:18.780
and they do that with
the people around them.

00:45:18.780 --> 00:45:21.330
And it's so effective at getting
people to bond and converse

00:45:21.330 --> 00:45:23.550
that we actually have to put a stop to it

00:45:23.550 --> 00:45:24.630
after a couple of minutes.

00:45:24.630 --> 00:45:27.780
The bottom line is this
disclosure deepens relationships

00:45:27.780 --> 00:45:30.810
and connections with others,
and that's why we consider it

00:45:30.810 --> 00:45:34.200
one of the most important
positive communication behaviors.

00:45:34.200 --> 00:45:35.790
- Your next steps are simple.

00:45:35.790 --> 00:45:40.200
In the next 24 hours, apply these steps.

00:45:40.200 --> 00:45:43.860
Use any opportunity you have
to tell more of your personal

00:45:43.860 --> 00:45:46.980
or professional story whenever
it fits the situation.

00:45:46.980 --> 00:45:49.830
If you're feeling
courageous, share a foible,

00:45:49.830 --> 00:45:52.320
a small weakness, so
that people can connect

00:45:52.320 --> 00:45:53.247
with the real you.

00:45:53.247 --> 00:45:55.950
And more importantly, if
you hear someone disclose,

00:45:55.950 --> 00:45:58.530
remember, that that's
the magic of disclosure,

00:45:58.530 --> 00:46:03.030
that you can reciprocate and
share your very own story.

