friend, Dr. Julien Mirivel.

00:46:52.260 --> 00:46:53.827
We co-authored a book together called,

00:46:53.827 --> 00:46:56.430
"Positive Communication for Leaders".

00:46:56.430 --> 00:46:59.160
That's the basis of this
video series we're doing.

00:46:59.160 --> 00:47:01.320
In fact, this is video number six

00:47:01.320 --> 00:47:04.830
in a seven-part series that
follows along with the book.

00:47:04.830 --> 00:47:07.020
If you'd like to, you can
check out that book on Amazon,

00:47:07.020 --> 00:47:10.290
and you can also check out
our nine-day mini email class

00:47:10.290 --> 00:47:12.150
that follows and reinforces

00:47:12.150 --> 00:47:13.920
what we're talking about in these videos.

00:47:13.920 --> 00:47:15.780
And today's topic is encouragement.

00:47:15.780 --> 00:47:18.060
Encouragement is a key leadership behavior

00:47:18.060 --> 00:47:19.650
in the model of communication

00:47:19.650 --> 00:47:21.270
that Julien developed years back.

00:47:21.270 --> 00:47:23.010
And today, you're gonna
learn how to create

00:47:23.010 --> 00:47:25.440
extraordinary moments with your people

00:47:25.440 --> 00:47:28.110
through the power of encouragement.

00:47:28.110 --> 00:47:31.170
People need their leaders
to encourage them now

00:47:31.170 --> 00:47:32.610
more than ever.

00:47:32.610 --> 00:47:34.500
- In the wheel of positive communication,

00:47:34.500 --> 00:47:38.160
we describe the power of
encouragement as the ability

00:47:38.160 --> 00:47:41.970
to give people the support
they need to succeed.

00:47:41.970 --> 00:47:45.000
When we encourage, we give social support.

00:47:45.000 --> 00:47:47.790
Consider some statistics that
we bring out in the book.

00:47:47.790 --> 00:47:52.080
For example, according to a
recent article by Jason Kanov

00:47:52.080 --> 00:47:56.310
and his research team, "Employee's
grief cost US businesses

00:47:56.310 --> 00:47:59.340
about $75 billion annually."

00:47:59.340 --> 00:48:03.060
And job stress and burnout
have been estimated to cost

00:48:03.060 --> 00:48:05.910
to industry hundreds
of billions of dollars.

00:48:05.910 --> 00:48:09.570
Similarly, we know today that
employees feel very lonely.

00:48:09.570 --> 00:48:13.590
In fact, a recent poll revealed
that 8 out of 10 employees,

00:48:13.590 --> 00:48:18.000
that's 82%, say that they
feel lonely in the workplace.

00:48:18.000 --> 00:48:19.020
- Now, on the flip side,

00:48:19.020 --> 00:48:22.440
we know that a little
compassion goes a long way.

00:48:22.440 --> 00:48:24.090
For example, when leaders demonstrate

00:48:24.090 --> 00:48:25.770
a preference for compassion,

00:48:25.770 --> 00:48:29.520
their followers are 25%
more engaged in their jobs,

00:48:29.520 --> 00:48:34.520
20% more committed, and at
an 11% lower risk of burnout.

00:48:34.980 --> 00:48:37.830
Leaders who show compassion
also personally benefit

00:48:37.830 --> 00:48:39.990
as they experience a 30% increase

00:48:39.990 --> 00:48:42.240
in their own sense of
wellbeing and happiness

00:48:42.240 --> 00:48:43.440
in their lives.

00:48:43.440 --> 00:48:46.050
In other words, your
encouragement, your compassion,

00:48:46.050 --> 00:48:48.660
your support make a measurable difference

00:48:48.660 --> 00:48:50.430
in everybody's lives.

00:48:50.430 --> 00:48:54.180
- In short, all employees
and followers need to feel

00:48:54.180 --> 00:48:57.210
that they're cared for, they
need to feel attended to,

00:48:57.210 --> 00:48:58.800
but they also need to be reminded

00:48:58.800 --> 00:49:01.500
of why we do the things we do.

00:49:01.500 --> 00:49:02.910
So how can you, as a leader,

00:49:02.910 --> 00:49:05.610
supply the power of positive communication

00:49:05.610 --> 00:49:09.840
to support your employees and
transform ordinary moments

00:49:09.840 --> 00:49:13.197
into what Alex and I see
as extraordinary ones?

00:49:13.197 --> 00:49:15.210
And the short answer is this,

00:49:15.210 --> 00:49:19.500
take every opportunity you have
to encourage your employees

00:49:19.500 --> 00:49:23.760
with any of these easy and
practical three strategies,

00:49:23.760 --> 00:49:26.070
plus a bonus one that we're
gonna share after those.

00:49:26.070 --> 00:49:30.210
The first one is this, give
sincere and meaningful advice

00:49:30.210 --> 00:49:33.750
to support your employees
in one-on-one moments.

00:49:33.750 --> 00:49:36.060
I had a great boss once who years ago

00:49:36.060 --> 00:49:38.850
was having lunch with me to mentor me.

00:49:38.850 --> 00:49:41.130
And for a long time, I was a smoker.

00:49:41.130 --> 00:49:42.690
And in that conversation,

00:49:42.690 --> 00:49:44.700
give me some advice that stuck with me.

00:49:44.700 --> 00:49:46.380
He said, "You know Jillian,
how you quit smoking,

00:49:46.380 --> 00:49:47.213
don't you?"

00:49:47.213 --> 00:49:48.900
And I said, "No, what
are you talking about?"

00:49:48.900 --> 00:49:52.230
And he said, "If you really
want to quit smoking,

00:49:52.230 --> 00:49:55.800
you have to learn to quit
one cigarette at a time."

00:49:55.800 --> 00:49:57.540
And it took a while for this encouragement

00:49:57.540 --> 00:50:00.600
and advice to sink in,
but about two weeks later,

00:50:00.600 --> 00:50:02.400
I decided to quit smoking,

00:50:02.400 --> 00:50:05.640
and I've been smoke-free
now for over 16 years.

00:50:05.640 --> 00:50:08.220
One piece of advice made a huge difference

00:50:08.220 --> 00:50:11.310
and encouraged me to do what
I wanted to do to begin with.

00:50:11.310 --> 00:50:13.860
- And it certainly
remains a memorable moment

00:50:13.860 --> 00:50:17.280
as you can see, because Julien
told me about it years ago,

00:50:17.280 --> 00:50:20.370
and reminded me of it as
we were writing this book,

00:50:20.370 --> 00:50:23.400
the second strategy is
to alleviate distress

00:50:23.400 --> 00:50:25.020
by providing support.

00:50:25.020 --> 00:50:27.690
So I used to be the chair
of my academic department.

00:50:27.690 --> 00:50:29.460
I teach college full-time still.

00:50:29.460 --> 00:50:31.500
And for about four years,

00:50:31.500 --> 00:50:34.500
I was the chair or
supervisor of my department.

00:50:34.500 --> 00:50:38.070
I was really burned out
at the end of that time.

00:50:38.070 --> 00:50:40.410
But my new chair, my new supervisor,

00:50:40.410 --> 00:50:42.690
was amazing at providing support.

00:50:42.690 --> 00:50:44.130
He sensed that I was burned out.

00:50:44.130 --> 00:50:47.640
He gave me an amazing teaching
schedule, really ideal,

00:50:47.640 --> 00:50:50.010
so that I would have a
little more free time.

00:50:50.010 --> 00:50:53.580
And he also delegated some
of the heavy responsibilities

00:50:53.580 --> 00:50:56.730
that I had been carrying to
other people to rotate that,

00:50:56.730 --> 00:50:58.320
so that other people could handle 'em

00:50:58.320 --> 00:50:59.880
to alleviate my distress.

00:50:59.880 --> 00:51:03.510
And fast forward to this
year, I feel refreshed,

00:51:03.510 --> 00:51:06.180
and I'm having my best
teaching year I've had

00:51:06.180 --> 00:51:09.780
in a long time, and he did
this by providing support.

00:51:09.780 --> 00:51:11.520
He alleviated my distress,

00:51:11.520 --> 00:51:12.960
and that was an incredibly supportive,

00:51:12.960 --> 00:51:15.300
very practical way that he helped me.

00:51:15.300 --> 00:51:18.600
- The third strategy is
to help your employees

00:51:18.600 --> 00:51:21.600
refocus on their strength
after they have failed

00:51:21.600 --> 00:51:23.640
or after they've encountered a setback.

00:51:23.640 --> 00:51:24.960
With the book that we wrote,

00:51:24.960 --> 00:51:28.140
Alex and I interviewed a
professional who told us a story

00:51:28.140 --> 00:51:30.660
about a time where she had
made a big mistake at work.

00:51:30.660 --> 00:51:32.730
So the first thing she did
is she called her boss,

00:51:32.730 --> 00:51:34.950
and admitted to all the
things that had happened

00:51:34.950 --> 00:51:37.170
as a result of some of
the actions that she took.

00:51:37.170 --> 00:51:39.030
Her boss on the phone after listening

00:51:39.030 --> 00:51:41.520
to all of her concerns
and solving the problems,

00:51:41.520 --> 00:51:42.360
then told her this.

00:51:42.360 --> 00:51:45.540
She said, "Analiese, you're
really good at what you do.

00:51:45.540 --> 00:51:48.270
You are good at your job, and you care,

00:51:48.270 --> 00:51:51.780
and you can overcome any
challenge, no problem."

00:51:51.780 --> 00:51:53.880
That encouragement made a huge world

00:51:53.880 --> 00:51:55.230
of difference in Analiese,

00:51:55.230 --> 00:51:57.270
and helped her overcome the failure

00:51:57.270 --> 00:51:59.340
that she had just encountered.

00:51:59.340 --> 00:52:02.340
- These are all small,
but thoughtful behaviors

00:52:02.340 --> 00:52:04.950
that you can do to take difficult moments,

00:52:04.950 --> 00:52:08.310
and turn them into fond
and extraordinary memories,

00:52:08.310 --> 00:52:10.320
just like the memories that we have.

00:52:10.320 --> 00:52:13.710
So fourth, your bonus strategy
is the encouraging power

00:52:13.710 --> 00:52:15.750
of your presence.

00:52:15.750 --> 00:52:18.720
Simply being available,
simply being present,

00:52:18.720 --> 00:52:21.210
and giving another person your time

00:52:21.210 --> 00:52:22.560
is, sometimes, all that's needed,

00:52:22.560 --> 00:52:25.020
and it may in fact be all that you can do.

00:52:25.020 --> 00:52:27.840
Not every difficulty or discouragement

00:52:27.840 --> 00:52:29.730
that someone's going
through can be something

00:52:29.730 --> 00:52:31.710
that you can fix and do something about

00:52:31.710 --> 00:52:32.910
on a practical level.

00:52:32.910 --> 00:52:36.120
However, you can make a
difference just by being there

00:52:36.120 --> 00:52:39.780
to offer your time,
attention, and support.

00:52:39.780 --> 00:52:42.180
Sometimes, that's the biggest
difference that you can make,

00:52:42.180 --> 00:52:44.460
simply to make yourself available,

00:52:44.460 --> 00:52:46.920
and let them know that
you are there for them.

00:52:46.920 --> 00:52:48.300
- Let's get practical.

00:52:48.300 --> 00:52:50.640
Here's your challenge for this video.

00:52:50.640 --> 00:52:53.250
Identify one employee or a colleague

00:52:53.250 --> 00:52:55.800
who seems to be a little
disconnected from work,

00:52:55.800 --> 00:52:58.410
maybe even disengaged from the mission.

00:52:58.410 --> 00:52:59.910
Reach out to them personally,

00:52:59.910 --> 00:53:02.910
and make some time to interact one-on-one.

00:53:02.910 --> 00:53:05.430
Third step, make sure
to ask lots of questions

00:53:05.430 --> 00:53:07.230
about their experiences at work,

00:53:07.230 --> 00:53:09.240
and to listen as deeply as you can

00:53:09.240 --> 00:53:10.650
to what they're going through.

00:53:10.650 --> 00:53:13.620
When it's appropriate, then
use that time to your offer

00:53:13.620 --> 00:53:16.590
one meaningful advice
or meaningful fitting

00:53:16.590 --> 00:53:18.270
piece of encouragement.

00:53:18.270 --> 00:53:21.090
- And remember, sometimes,
just your supportive

00:53:21.090 --> 00:53:24.420
and encouraging presence
will be the very best thing

00:53:24.420 --> 00:53:27.270
that you can offer someone
during times of difficulty.

00:53:27.270 --> 00:53:28.950
Those are the kinds of memories

00:53:28.950 --> 00:53:31.380
that they may carry with
them for a long time.

