00:55:06.900 --> 00:55:08.400
Anyway, back to our story,

00:55:08.400 --> 00:55:10.770
we're going to talk about listening.

00:55:10.770 --> 00:55:12.900
It's a key leadership behavior,

00:55:12.900 --> 00:55:15.990
and Julien's going to
talk about this skill

00:55:15.990 --> 00:55:17.520
from the perspective of his model

00:55:17.520 --> 00:55:19.920
that he created on positive communication.

00:55:19.920 --> 00:55:22.290
- When you choose to listen as a leader,

00:55:22.290 --> 00:55:25.470
you are choosing to transcend
the perceived differences

00:55:25.470 --> 00:55:28.020
that exist between you and other people.

00:55:28.020 --> 00:55:29.520
Why is listening such an important

00:55:29.520 --> 00:55:31.110
leadership behavior, Alex?

00:55:31.110 --> 00:55:33.210
- But listening is an often overlooked,

00:55:33.210 --> 00:55:35.910
and usually undervalued
communication skill.

00:55:35.910 --> 00:55:38.220
It often doesn't look
like anything's happening,

00:55:38.220 --> 00:55:40.350
but if you want to take
your leadership seriously,

00:55:40.350 --> 00:55:42.240
then you have to work on your listening,

00:55:42.240 --> 00:55:45.810
because the higher up leaders
go in any organization

00:55:45.810 --> 00:55:47.970
the more time they spend listening.

00:55:47.970 --> 00:55:49.560
In fact, as we show in our book,

00:55:49.560 --> 00:55:52.170
executives spend twice
as much time listening

00:55:52.170 --> 00:55:54.120
compared to the average employee.

00:55:54.120 --> 00:55:57.300
And emerging leaders spend
more and more time listening

00:55:57.300 --> 00:56:01.800
as they move up the ladder
in any kind of organization.

00:56:01.800 --> 00:56:04.140
And people in all walks of life,

00:56:04.140 --> 00:56:05.550
whether they're in an organization

00:56:05.550 --> 00:56:09.630
or in their personal life,
love great listeners.

00:56:09.630 --> 00:56:12.390
- But there are real
important practical benefits

00:56:12.390 --> 00:56:14.430
to being a great listener.

00:56:14.430 --> 00:56:17.010
Leaders who listen well and deeply,

00:56:17.010 --> 00:56:19.080
get to know their people better.

00:56:19.080 --> 00:56:21.780
They also can be more easily identifying

00:56:21.780 --> 00:56:24.810
the emerging problems that are
taking place on the ground.

00:56:24.810 --> 00:56:27.030
They have more input about solutions

00:56:27.030 --> 00:56:30.450
that could be more effective
to solve real problems.

00:56:30.450 --> 00:56:32.730
They also are really good
if you use listening,

00:56:32.730 --> 00:56:35.790
you can resolve the problems
before they get bigger.

00:56:35.790 --> 00:56:37.200
And more importantly, most of us,

00:56:37.200 --> 00:56:39.450
we want to avoid hard
conversations, right?

00:56:39.450 --> 00:56:40.740
Difficult moments.

00:56:40.740 --> 00:56:43.170
If you can listen a
little bit more deeply,

00:56:43.170 --> 00:56:44.880
you can avoid these hard talks,

00:56:44.880 --> 00:56:47.850
because you're managing
problems all along the way.

00:56:47.850 --> 00:56:50.550
For these reasons, it doesn't surprise us

00:56:50.550 --> 00:56:53.640
that some of the best
listeners we've ever met

00:56:53.640 --> 00:56:57.150
are leaders sitting at the top
of the organizational chart.

00:56:57.150 --> 00:56:59.460
- So what does good listening look like,

00:56:59.460 --> 00:57:01.470
sound like, and feel like?

00:57:01.470 --> 00:57:03.690
Well, in our book, we use
the term "deep listening"

00:57:03.690 --> 00:57:06.750
to indicate that
listening well goes beyond

00:57:06.750 --> 00:57:09.510
superficial tactics like fake listening

00:57:09.510 --> 00:57:11.700
to give people the impression

00:57:11.700 --> 00:57:13.500
that you're listening when you're not.

00:57:13.500 --> 00:57:17.670
For example, deep listeners
follow three key behaviors.

00:57:17.670 --> 00:57:19.800
First, deep listeners are fully present

00:57:19.800 --> 00:57:22.470
and completely in the moment.

00:57:22.470 --> 00:57:24.720
Deep listeners put their phones away.

00:57:24.720 --> 00:57:26.640
They reduce all distractions.

00:57:26.640 --> 00:57:28.590
They literally lean their bodies in,

00:57:28.590 --> 00:57:32.280
and they listen with their
eyes, not just their ears.

00:57:32.280 --> 00:57:33.660
Deep listeners, for example,

00:57:33.660 --> 00:57:35.850
are never looking over
your shoulder to see

00:57:35.850 --> 00:57:38.910
who else might be a little
more interesting in the room.

00:57:38.910 --> 00:57:41.100
And a fully present
listener makes you feel

00:57:41.100 --> 00:57:43.470
like you're the most important person

00:57:43.470 --> 00:57:45.450
in the world at that moment.

00:57:45.450 --> 00:57:48.330
They're fully present,
and that is a choice.

00:57:48.330 --> 00:57:50.940
For example, they say that Elvis Presley

00:57:50.940 --> 00:57:53.400
made you feel like you were
the only person that existed

00:57:53.400 --> 00:57:56.730
in the entire universe while
he was speaking to you.

00:57:56.730 --> 00:57:58.200
He made you feel special.

00:57:58.200 --> 00:58:00.570
And if the king of rock and roll

00:58:00.570 --> 00:58:03.810
had the time to make people
feel that way, so can you.

00:58:03.810 --> 00:58:06.720
- Deep listeners are also open.

00:58:06.720 --> 00:58:09.360
You know, openness is
about welcoming new ideas,

00:58:09.360 --> 00:58:11.220
perspectives, and approaches.

00:58:11.220 --> 00:58:13.260
We know that remaining open is difficult,

00:58:13.260 --> 00:58:15.960
but it does not mean
abandoning your point of view.

00:58:15.960 --> 00:58:18.450
In fact, openness is all about maintaining

00:58:18.450 --> 00:58:20.460
a really fine, delicate balance

00:58:20.460 --> 00:58:23.310
between holding onto your own perspective,

00:58:23.310 --> 00:58:27.060
and being profoundly open to
the perspective of others.

00:58:27.060 --> 00:58:29.730
Openness is typically difficult
for people to develop,

00:58:29.730 --> 00:58:32.310
because it involves our ability to accept

00:58:32.310 --> 00:58:33.930
and welcome new information,

00:58:33.930 --> 00:58:36.390
which may even contradict
our own thinking.

00:58:36.390 --> 00:58:38.940
We interviewed a variety
of leaders for the book

00:58:38.940 --> 00:58:41.917
that Alex and I wrote,
but one of them said this,

00:58:41.917 --> 00:58:44.610
"Leaders, in general, are very stubborn.

00:58:44.610 --> 00:58:47.280
They believe in something
and they keep on pushing

00:58:47.280 --> 00:58:50.160
whether people agree or disagree."

00:58:50.160 --> 00:58:52.920
But he added this important
piece of information.

00:58:52.920 --> 00:58:56.250
He said, "But I have found
that listening deeply

00:58:56.250 --> 00:58:59.250
always gives me a new perspective."

00:58:59.250 --> 00:59:00.870
So gaining a fresh perspective,

00:59:00.870 --> 00:59:03.630
even if one may not
fully change their view,

00:59:03.630 --> 00:59:05.580
is obviously critical for leaders.

00:59:05.580 --> 00:59:08.340
So as a leader, we
encourage you to remain open

00:59:08.340 --> 00:59:11.250
to hearing new ideas,
to hearing new thoughts,

00:59:11.250 --> 00:59:13.290
and constructs that
will help you innovate,

00:59:13.290 --> 00:59:15.720
adapt, and hopefully thrive.

00:59:15.720 --> 00:59:19.260
- The third behavior is that
deep listeners show empathy.

00:59:19.260 --> 00:59:22.100
Put simply empathy is your
ability to put yourself

00:59:22.100 --> 00:59:25.350
in the other person's shoes
to understand their world,

00:59:25.350 --> 00:59:28.140
and to feel what they are
feeling in that moment.

00:59:28.140 --> 00:59:30.990
Empathy usually involves pitching back

00:59:30.990 --> 00:59:32.910
what you believe you have heard,

00:59:32.910 --> 00:59:35.550
or asking a few follow-up
questions to make sure

00:59:35.550 --> 00:59:37.140
you are understanding the other person's

00:59:37.140 --> 00:59:39.420
point of view accurately.

00:59:39.420 --> 00:59:41.940
So I'm gonna give you a few
straightforward examples

00:59:41.940 --> 00:59:44.580
of how you might respond in the moment,

00:59:44.580 --> 00:59:46.470
and we're gonna be really
straightforward here.

00:59:46.470 --> 00:59:48.330
When good listeners respond,

00:59:48.330 --> 00:59:51.810
they keep their responses low key.

00:59:51.810 --> 00:59:54.157
For example, they may say things like,

00:59:54.157 --> 00:59:55.297
"I hear you."

00:59:55.297 --> 00:59:56.557
"Makes sense."

00:59:56.557 --> 00:59:57.930
"I see what you mean."

00:59:57.930 --> 01:00:00.090
Or even simply, "Hm."

01:00:00.090 --> 01:00:02.790
You'll notice that these
replies are all very short.

01:00:02.790 --> 01:00:04.470
Great listeners are not eager

01:00:04.470 --> 01:00:06.720
to take their own big talking turn,

01:00:06.720 --> 01:00:09.900
or to jump in with a
story to match your story.

01:00:09.900 --> 01:00:12.750
They're entirely focused
on understanding you

01:00:12.750 --> 01:00:15.030
and understanding your world.

01:00:15.030 --> 01:00:16.687
As Stephen Covey might say,

01:00:16.687 --> 01:00:19.380
"Great listeners listen to understand;

01:00:19.380 --> 01:00:22.050
they don't listen to simply reply."

01:00:22.050 --> 01:00:24.660
- Let's get actionable,
here is your challenge.

01:00:24.660 --> 01:00:28.170
This is a simple action
step, but it takes commitment

01:00:28.170 --> 01:00:32.040
and courage on your part
to execute this challenge.

01:00:32.040 --> 01:00:36.300
In the next 24 hours,
strive to make listening

01:00:36.300 --> 01:00:40.050
your dominant goal in any
conversation that you're having.

01:00:40.050 --> 01:00:41.880
It's gonna be your purpose.

01:00:41.880 --> 01:00:43.800
Use the tips that we've
shared in this video

01:00:43.800 --> 01:00:46.740
to listen more effectively
and more deeply.

01:00:46.740 --> 01:00:49.320
Remember that it's a part of your job.

01:00:49.320 --> 01:00:50.580
Here's a quick summary.

01:00:50.580 --> 01:00:54.360
Recognize that listening is
a leadership responsibility.

01:00:54.360 --> 01:00:58.350
And deep listening means that
you become fully present,

01:00:58.350 --> 01:01:02.220
that you become fully open,
and that you become empathetic.

01:01:02.220 --> 01:01:04.890
We know that listening in
one-on-one conversations

01:01:04.890 --> 01:01:06.240
is foundational.

01:01:06.240 --> 01:01:08.580
And in our book, we also
explain how you can use

01:01:08.580 --> 01:01:12.060
the power of listening to
build an extraordinary team

01:01:12.060 --> 01:01:14.280
and a great organizational culture,

01:01:14.280 --> 01:01:16.650
heart and centered on compassion.

